window.RT_DOCS = [{"t":"Reqtide PHP documentation","u":"index.html","d":"Documentation for Reqtide PHP, a resilience toolkit for code that calls unreliable APIs: one pipeline with a total deadline, retries, rate limiting, a circuit breaker, a bulkhead and fallbacks. Alpha. The core package keeps state in one process.","h":[],"x":""},{"t":"All guides","u":"guides/index.html","d":"Every Reqtide PHP guide in reading order, with a filter: install, concepts, each protection, errors, testing, the API reference, limits and a comparison.","h":[],"x":""},{"t":"Install","u":"guides/install.html","d":"Install Reqtide PHP from a clone with a Composer path repository, or run the examples in Docker. Packagist is not available yet.","h":[["what-works-today","What works today"],["requirements","Requirements"],["install-from-a-clone","Install from a clone"],["try-it-without-installing-php","Try it without installing PHP"],["check-that-it-works","Check that it works"],["read-these-docs-offline","Read these docs offline"],["next","Next"]],"x":"Alpha The package enaxon/reqtide is not on Packagist, so composer require enaxon/reqtide does not work yet. There is no tagged release. What works today Route Status Notes Composer path repository, from a clone Works Checked on 2026-10-09 with Composer 2 on PHP 8.3 in Docker. This is the route below. Composer VCS repository (straight from GitHub) Not checked Needs the public repository to contain the package layout. Use the path repository until this page says otherwise. Packagist ( composer require enaxon/reqtide ) Not available The package is not submitted. PHP 8.3, 8.4, 8.5 Checked in Docker Command line only. See Limits and non-guarantees . PHP-FPM, Apache, other web servers Not run State is process-local, so read Limits and non-guarantees before you use it in a web request. enaxon/reqtide-redis and enaxon/reqtide-guzzle Development repository only Separate packages, not part of the archive of enaxon/reqtide . Not released, not on Packagist, run only in local Docker. Install steps are in Shared state with Redis and Guzzle requests with Reqtide . See also Limits and non-guarantees . Requirements PHP 8.3 or newer on a 64-bit build ( php-64bit ). Composer 2. No other runtime dependencies. composer.json requires only php and php-64bit . Install from a clone Step 1: Clone the repository Clone it next to your project: Step 2: Register the clone as a path repository In your project's composer.json , add the repository. symlink: false makes Composer copy the package into vendor/ instead of linking it: Step 3: Require the package The vendor name is enaxon . A different Packagist vendor, naxon , is one letter apart: copy the package name exactly and check the vendor before you run composer require . One package, enaxon/reqtide , contains the library ( Enaxon\\Reqtide ) and the test helpers ( Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing ). The helpers are installed with it and are meant for tests only. The archive you install holds src/ , docs/ , composer.json , README.md , LICENSE and CHANGELOG.md . Tests, tools and examples stay in the repository. Try it without installing PHP If you have Docker, run the examples inside the clone. No PHP or Composer is needed on your machine: The output is: Use php84 or php85 in place of php83 to try the other versions. Check that it works Create demo.php in your project with the script from Getting started and run: You should see the two lines above. If PHP reports Class \"Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Pipeline\\PipelineBuilder\" not found , the autoloader line is missing or points to the wrong vendor/ folder. If Composer reports that it cannot find enaxon/reqtide , check the url of the path repository. Read these docs offline The docs/ folder is a static site with no build step. Open docs/index.html in a browser, or serve it: Next Getting started builds your first pipeline. Limits and non-guarantees lists what the alpha does not do. Next Getting started"},{"t":"Getting started","u":"guides/getting-started.html","d":"Build your first protected call: a retry, a retry budget, a rate limit, a circuit breaker and a total deadline, line by line.","h":[["step-1-load-the-classes","Step 1: Load the classes"],["step-2-say-which-failure-is-transient","Step 2: Say which failure is transient"],["step-3-build-the-pipeline-once","Step 3: Build the pipeline once"],["step-4-write-the-call-you-want-to-protect","Step 4: Write the call you want to protect"],["step-5-run-it-with-a-context","Step 5: Run it with a context"],["when-something-is-wrong","When something is wrong"],["limits-to-remember","Limits to remember"],["next-steps","Next steps"]],"x":"Alpha Reqtide is not released and not on Packagist. State is process-local and in-memory only. See Limits . Time: about 10 minutes. You need: PHP 8.3 or newer (64-bit), Composer, and the package installed as described in Install . Every code block below is part of examples/06-getting-started.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. Step 1: Load the classes In your project the last line loads Composer's autoloader. In this repository the example loads it through examples/autoload.php . Step 2: Say which failure is transient Reqtide never guesses which of your exceptions are safe to retry. You name the classes in the next step. Anything you do not name is a plain failure: it is not retried, it is not replaced by a fallback, and it counts against the circuit breaker. Step 3: Build the pipeline once Each line is one decision, and every number is yours because Reqtide ships no defaults: Call Decides profile('payments', 'v1') The name and version of this configuration. The version appears in every error report. partition('shop', 'prod', 'payments-api') Whose state this is: application, environment and the dependency being protected. State is keyed by it, so give each dependency its own subject. attemptTimeout(...) The longest one attempt may take. Cooperative states the truth: the synchronous runtime cannot stop your code, so your code must respect the time. retry(3, ...) At most 3 attempts including the first, backing off exponentially from 10 ms to at most 100 ms with full jitter. retryBudget(10, 10, 60 s, 10 s) A shared pool of 10 retry tokens refilled by 10 per minute, so a dependency that is down does not receive three times the traffic. It is required whenever retry() allows more than one attempt. rateLimit(tokenBucket(5, 5, 1 s)) A token bucket of capacity 5 refilled by 5 tokens per second. Every dispatch costs one token, the first attempt and each retry alike. circuitBreaker(lastCalls(10), 4, 1/2, 30 s, 1, 1, 10 s) Judge the last 10 completed calls once 4 exist, open at a failure share of at least 1/2, stay open for 30 s, then allow 1 probe; one successful probe closes it. The last argument is how long a permit stays valid, and it must exceed the attempt timeout by more than 1 s. compileInMemory(...) Validates the combination and wires process-local memory backends. A mistake raises InvalidConfigurationException naming the option. Build the pipeline at start-up and reuse it. It holds the state that makes the limits work, so a pipeline you rebuild per request forgets everything each time. Step 4: Write the call you want to protect Your callable receives an AttemptContext (operation id, attempt number, the deadline). Return the value; throw your own exceptions. This one fails twice and then succeeds, so you can watch the retries. Step 5: Run it with a context Run it. The output is: What happened: newContext() created the deadline: 30 seconds for the whole operation, retries and waits included. It also carries your statement that repeating this call is safe. forReadOnlyCallable() is that statement. Reqtide cannot verify it and will not retry without it. Attempts 1 and 2 threw UpstreamTimeout , which you listed as retryable. Each time the pipeline waited a backoff delay, drew one token from the retry budget and charged one rate-limit token. Attempt 3 returned a value, and execute() returned exactly that value. Its type is known to static analysis: string . The three dispatches consumed three rate-limit tokens. A charge is never refunded, so size quotas for the expected traffic multiplied by the attempts. When something is wrong Two mistakes you are likely to make first, with the messages Reqtide gives (see error handling for the full model): The first is raised while building; the second when a retrying pipeline gets a context without the assertion. Both come before anything is sent. Limits to remember State is in the memory of this PHP process. Under PHP-FPM a pipeline built inside a request starts every request with a full quota and a closed circuit. Build it once per long-lived worker, or accept per-request limits. Shared state needs the separate Redis package, which is not released (see Shared state with Redis and Limits ). The deadline is checked between steps. It does not interrupt your callable. The example's delays really sleep, in slices of at most 5 ms. For tests that must not sleep, see testing . Next steps Concepts : the pipeline, the operation, the deadline and the partition. Choosing policies : what each protection is for and what it refuses. Error handling : catch refusals, read ReasonCode and Problem , use detailed mode. Testing with the virtual clock : virtual time, scripted attempts, recorded events. Previous Install Next Concepts"},{"t":"Concepts","u":"guides/concepts.html","d":"The pipeline you build once, the operation and its attempts, the one deadline that covers them, and the partition whose state they share.","h":[["four-things-reqtide-keeps-apart","Four things Reqtide keeps apart"],["the-pipeline-is-built-once","The pipeline is built once"],["what-happens-to-one-attempt","What happens to one attempt"],["an-operation-has-one-deadline-and-many-attempts","An operation has one deadline and many attempts"],["the-deadline","The deadline"],["the-partition","The partition"],["what-counts-as-a-failure-is-your-decision","What counts as a failure is your decision"],["where-to-go-next","Where to go next"]],"x":"Alpha State is process-local and in-memory only, and a running callback is never interrupted. See Limits and non-guarantees . Every code block below comes from examples/10-concepts.php or examples/09-choosing-policies.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. Four things Reqtide keeps apart Thing What it is Lifetime Pipeline The configuration (policies and their numbers) plus the state those policies keep: rate-limit tokens, circuit history, retry budget. Built once, reused for every call. Operation One logical piece of work you want done, such as \"load this order\". It has one context, one total deadline and one result. One call to execute() . Attempt One physical call of your callable. An operation makes one attempt, or several when retries are allowed. One dispatch. Partition Whose state this is: application, environment, subject (and optionally a tenant). As long as the pipeline lives. Keeping them apart is what lets one pipeline serve many concurrent operations without them sharing any per-call data, and what lets a deadline cover all the attempts of one operation. The pipeline is built once PipelineBuilder collects your options and validates the combination. compileInMemory() wires process-local state and returns a CompiledPipeline . The builder has no defaults: a policy exists only if you call its method, and every number is yours. explain() describes what you built without calling anything, touching state or doing I/O. Use it for a start-up log line or to assert in a test that a pipeline is what you think it is: Read the list of enabled policies first: retry(maxAttempts=1) is always present and means \"no retries\" until you call retry() . The capability notes are the honest part. attempt_timeout=unsupported and physical_stop=not_guaranteed say that the synchronous runtime cannot stop your callable, and scheduling=bounded_blocking says that retry delays really sleep. Build the pipeline at start-up and reuse it. It holds the state that makes limits work, so a pipeline you rebuild for every request forgets everything each time. It has no per-operation state, so it is safe to share between operations in one process. What happens to one attempt For each attempt the pipeline asks its questions in a fixed order: Order Check If it says no 1 The deadline and the context The operation ends with deadline_exceeded or operation_cancelled . 2 Retry eligibility (for attempts after the first) Your last result is returned. 3 The minimum remaining allowance for the attempt (attempts after the first) Your last result is returned. 4 The retry budget, which reserves one token (attempts after the first) retry_budget_exhausted is recorded and your last result is returned. 5 The circuit breaker circuit_open , and your callable does not run. 6 The bulkhead concurrency_capacity_exhausted . 7 The shared cooldown cooldown_active or cooldown_exceeds_deadline . 8 The rate limit rate.exhausted or a related code. 9 Dispatch Your callable runs. If a later check refuses, the permits the earlier checks took are released: a circuit probe is abandoned without counting, the bulkhead slot is freed and a reserved retry token is released. Nothing is held while the pipeline waits between retries. A refusal never reaches your callable and is not counted as a failure of your dependency. An operation has one deadline and many attempts You build one context per operation with newContext() . It carries the total deadline, your statement about repeating the call, an optional fallback and an operation id. The pipeline gives your callable an AttemptContext on every attempt. What this shows: The operation id is the same on all three attempts, and each attempt has its own attempt id. Logs that name the operation id group the attempts of one operation. dispatchedAttemptNumber counts physical dispatches. This is the number to put in a log line. You build the context right before the call, not at start-up. The deadline starts when newContext() builds it, so an old context would carry an almost spent budget. Never share one context between two operations: it names the operation. The deadline The timeout you give newContext() is the budget of the whole operation: all attempts, all waits between them and a fallback. It must be positive, because an already expired deadline can only fail. Every snippet in the protection guides starts from the same two required options. This is the setup the deadline example uses: The slow callable above ran to completion. The deadline is checked between steps, not inside your code, so a result that arrives late is replaced by deadline_exceeded ; the value itself stays available in the attempt records of detailed mode . Reqtide checks the deadline before each admission and dispatch, so an attempt does not start once the deadline has passed, and a retry whose delay does not fit in the remaining time is not started. Not a timeout for your code The deadline does not interrupt a running callable. The attempt timeout you declare with attemptTimeout() as Cooperative is a statement that your code respects the time, for example through a transport timeout. Reqtide cannot enforce it with the synchronous runtime. The partition A partition names whose state a pipeline keeps: an application, an environment, a subject (what you protect, for example catalog-api , never a credential) and optionally a tenant. All the policies of a pipeline share its partition. One pipeline serves exactly one partition. A context built by a pipeline for another partition is refused before anything is dispatched: Choose the subject to match the thing whose capacity you want to protect: one subject per dependency, one pipeline per tenant when limits are per tenant. In the in-memory setup every compiled pipeline owns its backends, so two pipelines never share a quota, even for the same partition. The profile id and version name the configuration. Change the version when the settings change, because state is keyed by it. What counts as a failure is your decision Policies act on the classification of each attempt, and your classifier decides what an outcome means: retryable, a circuit failure, replaceable by a fallback. ExceptionClassifier starts conservative. See Choosing policies . Where to go next Choosing policies maps problems to protections. Retry and budget and Rate limiting cover the two protections that interact most. Error handling explains what comes out when a protection says no. Previous Getting started Next Choosing policies"},{"t":"Choosing policies","u":"guides/choosing-policies.html","d":"Which protection answers which problem, how the classifier decides what counts as a failure, and the sizing rules Reqtide enforces. Start here, then read the guide for each protection.","h":[["which-problem-which-policy","Which problem, which policy"],["the-classifier-decides-what-counts","The classifier decides what counts"],["every-snippet-starts-the-same-way","Every snippet starts the same way"],["what-is-checked-in-which-order","What is checked, in which order"],["sizing-checklist","Sizing checklist"],["the-guides-one-per-protection","The guides, one per protection"]],"x":"Alpha Process-local, in-memory state only: every limit applies inside one PHP process. Under PHP-FPM a pipeline built inside a request starts every request fresh. See Limits and non-guarantees . Reqtide ships no defaults for timeouts, limits, windows or delays, and these guides recommend no numbers: the right values depend on your dependency, your traffic and your risk. Every code block in the protection guides comes from examples/09-choosing-policies.php or a sibling example, which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. The numbers in them make each protection visible in a few lines. They are not advice. Which problem, which policy Problem Policy Builder call Guide One slow call holds the caller for too long Total deadline, plus an attempt timeout newContext(timeout) , attemptTimeout() (both required) Concepts Brief failures that a second try may fix Retry with backoff, paid from a retry budget retry() , retryBudget() Retry and budget A provider's quota must not be exceeded Rate limit rateLimit() Rate limiting A dependency is down and callers keep hitting it Circuit breaker circuitBreaker() Circuit breaker One slow dependency uses up all concurrent work in the process Bulkhead bulkhead() Bulkhead and cooldown The server said \"retry after N seconds\" Retry-After as a lower bound, plus a shared cooldown ExceptionClassifier::retryAfterFrom() , cooldown() Retry and budget , Bulkhead and cooldown Callers need something useful when the call fails Fallback fallbackScope() and a fallback in newContext() Fallback You only pay for what you configure: a policy exists only if you call its method. The classifier decides what counts Policies act on the outcome of each attempt, and your classifier decides what an outcome means . ExceptionClassifier starts conservative: a returned value is a success; an exception is not retried and not replaced by a fallback unless you list its class ( retryOn() , fallbackOn() ); every exception counts as a circuit failure, unless you list its class in ignoreForCircuit() (for example the caller's own validation error) or narrow the failures with countAsCircuitFailure() . unacceptableValuesWhen() marks a returned value as a failure (a circuit failure, never retried, never replaced). retryAfterFrom() extracts a wait from an exception. The decision table is in the ExceptionClassifier docblock. Rate-limit, circuit, bulkhead and cooldown refusals, cancellations and storage errors are neutral: they never count as dependency failures. An exception nobody listed is not retried, because repeating an unexpected failure is a decision you must make explicitly. It is still counted as a circuit failure, because a circuit that ignored unknown failures would stay closed against a dependency that fails in a way nobody anticipated. Matching uses instanceof , so a listed parent class or interface covers its subclasses. Every snippet starts the same way The protection guides build their pipelines from two required options. The attempt timeout is required. Cooperative means your code respects the time; the synchronous runtime cannot stop it. The deadline you pass to newContext() is a separate, total budget for the whole operation. What is checked, in which order For each attempt: the deadline and context first, then retry eligibility, the minimum remaining allowance and the retry-budget reservation (the last two only for attempts after the first), then the circuit, the bulkhead, the shared cooldown and the rate limit, then the dispatch. If a later check refuses, the earlier permits are released. Nothing is held during a retry delay. Concepts shows the order as a table. Sizing checklist Reqtide validates the combination when you compile, and the messages name the option. These are the constraints it enforces, not recommended values: every lease ( bulkhead , circuit permit lease, retry-budget reservation lease) must exceed the attempt timeout by more than 1 second; retry() with more than one attempt needs retryBudget() , and a retryBudget() without it is rejected; circuit minimum samples cannot exceed a count window; the probe success threshold cannot exceed the probe capacity; fallbackScope(IncludeAdmissionRefusals) needs at least one admission policy. Everything else (limits, windows, delays, pool sizes) is a decision about your dependency. Measure it, record it, and test the pipeline under virtual time ( Testing with the virtual clock ). The guides, one per protection Retry and budget : attempts, backoff, jitter, the retry budget, Retry-After . Rate limiting : token bucket, fixed window, sliding counter, and what a charge means. Circuit breaker : windows, thresholds, probes. Bulkhead and cooldown : concurrency slots, leases and the shared cooldown. Fallback : what a fallback may replace, and what it never does. Previous Concepts Next Retry and budget"},{"t":"Retry and budget","u":"guides/retry-and-budget.html","d":"Retries with backoff and jitter, the safety statement every retry needs, the retry budget that stops a retry storm, and how Retry-After is honored or returned to you.","h":[["a-retry-needs-three-things","A retry needs three things"],["configure-the-retry-and-its-budget","Configure the retry and its budget"],["make-the-statement-on-every-call","Make the statement on every call"],["the-deadline-still-rules","The deadline still rules"],["when-the-retry-policy-gives-up","When the retry policy gives up"],["retry-after-honored-or-returned-to-you","Retry-After: honored, or returned to you"],["testing-retries","Testing retries"]],"x":"Alpha State is process-local: the retry budget belongs to one PHP process, and a running callback is never interrupted. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from examples/06-getting-started.php , examples/07-error-handling.php and examples/09-choosing-policies.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. A retry needs three things Reqtide never retries on its own initiative. A second attempt happens only when all three of these hold: Needed Why Where you give it Your statement that repeating the call is safe Reqtide cannot know whether a callable has side effects, and it has no HTTP knowledge. OperationSafety in newContext() A retry budget Retries must be paid for, so a failing dependency is not hammered. retryBudget() , required whenever retry() allows more than one attempt A retryable exception class You decide which of your failures are transient. ExceptionClassifier::retryOn() Without the statement, the context defaults to OperationSafety::unsafe() and a pipeline that allows more than one attempt refuses to run. An exception you did not list is a plain failure: it is returned to you, not retried. Configure the retry and its budget maxAttempts counts the first attempt, so 3 means one call plus at most two retries. It must be between 1 and 64. The delay grows from baseDelay , constant or exponential, and never exceeds maximumDelay . JitterMode::Full draws each delay uniformly from zero to the computed delay. JitterMode::None waits exactly the computed delay. The optional last argument is the time that must still remain after a delay for another attempt to be worth starting. The default is none. The retry budget is a token bucket: retryBudget(10, 10, Duration::seconds(60), Duration::seconds(10)) holds at most 10 tokens, adds 10 per minute, and holds a reservation for at most 10 seconds while a retry is being dispatched. That last number must exceed the attempt timeout by more than 1 second. The budget is an absolute pool of tokens, not a ratio of first attempts, and you size it. An oversized pool gives no protection. An undersized one stops retries early with retry_budget_exhausted . Make the statement on every call forReadOnlyCallable() is your assertion that running the callable again is safe. Reqtide cannot check it. The same call also takes how long the assertion stays valid. Two other kinds exist: providerIdempotentMutation() for a provider that deduplicates repeated mutations, and unsafe() , the default. Two mistakes you are likely to make first, with the messages Reqtide gives. Both are raised before anything is sent: The first is raised while building, the second when a retrying pipeline gets a context without the assertion. The deadline still rules A retry whose delay does not fit in the remaining deadline is not started, and the reason is retry_delay_exceeds_deadline . The total deadline covers all attempts and all waits. Nothing is held during a delay: no circuit permit, no bulkhead slot. Every retry also costs a rate-limit unit. See Rate limiting . When the retry policy gives up When retries stop because of the retry policy itself, your last exception is kept as the result, and the reason is recorded as a retry -stage problem. The simple execute() call throws your own exception. Detailed mode explains why it stopped: A refusal by another protection (circuit, bulkhead, rate limit, cooldown) on a later attempt behaves differently: the refusal becomes the result, and your last exception is attached as getPrevious() . Retry-After: honored, or returned to you A server-stated wait, such as the Retry-After header of an HTTP 429, is treated as a lower bound for the next attempt. Reqtide never sends sooner and never clamps the wait down. Reqtide has no HTTP awareness: turning a 429 and its header into a Duration is your classifier's job, with retryAfterFrom() . The class below stands in for the exception you would throw on a 429: When honoring the wait is impossible, the answer is a deferral, not a sleep : The wait exceeds the retry policy's maximumDelay : retry_after_exceeds_maximum_delay . The wait does not fit in the remaining deadline: retry_after_exceeds_deadline . The exception is an AdmissionDeferredException carrying the wait, with your throttling exception as getPrevious() . A positive Retry-After also extends a shared cooldown for the partition, so the next operation is deferred without calling the API. That is the subject of Bulkhead and cooldown . Testing retries A test of exponential backoff should not sleep. With the virtual clock, a scripted callable and a recorded event list you can assert the exact delays: see Testing with the virtual clock . Previous Choosing policies Next Rate limiting"},{"t":"Rate limiting","u":"guides/rate-limiting.html","d":"Token bucket, fixed window and an approximate sliding counter, what a charge means when retries are involved, and which refusal you get when the quota is spent.","h":[["three-algorithms-one-builder-call","Three algorithms, one builder call"],["what-a-charge-means","What a charge means"],["refusals-rejected-deferred-impossible","Refusals: rejected, deferred, impossible"],["a-fallback-for-refusals-is-a-choice","A fallback for refusals is a choice"],["testing-a-limit","Testing a limit"]],"x":"Alpha The limit protects one PHP process. Ten workers each get the full quota, and under PHP-FPM a pipeline built inside a request starts every request with a full quota. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from examples/09-choosing-policies.php and examples/11-rate-limit-charges.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. The numbers make behavior visible. They are not recommendations. Three algorithms, one builder call rateLimit(algorithm, cost) takes the algorithm from one of three factory methods. This snippet gives each algorithm room for exactly one call per hour and shows that the second call is refused: Shared setup for the snippets on this page Algorithm Behavior tokenBucket(capacity, refillTokens, interval) Admits while tokens remain; refills continuously in integer fixed-point; excess is discarded when full. fixedWindow(capacity, window, anchor) Counts per window. Aligned windows start at multiples of the window length on the epoch, FirstHit windows start at the first call. Exactly at the window end a new window starts. slidingWindow(capacity, window) An approximate sliding counter: it never admits early, but it can admit later than an exact sliding log would. All three use integer arithmetic, and time is monotonic: a clock that steps backwards never refills a quota. Which one to choose depends on what your provider enforces. If it documents a bucket, use a token bucket. If it resets a counter at fixed times, a fixed window with the matching anchor behaves the same way. The sliding counter is the cautious choice when you do not know the provider's rule, at the price of sometimes admitting later than necessary. What a charge means Every dispatch is charged cost units (default 1): the first attempt and each retry alike. A charge is never refunded, even for a call cancelled after admission and before dispatch. Quota used is therefore not the same as requests sent. This is the consequence for sizing. A bucket of 3 units with a retry policy of up to 3 attempts is emptied by one operation that retries twice: Size the quota for the expected traffic multiplied by the attempts you allow, not by the number of operations. Refusals: rejected, deferred, impossible A refusal reaches you as one of three typed exceptions. Your callable did not run, and the refusal is not counted as a failure of your dependency. You get Meaning AdmissionRejectedException The quota is spent. A wait may be known, or not, depending on the algorithm. AdmissionDeferredException The refusal carries a positive wait. ImpossibleAdmissionException The request can never succeed, for example because its cost is above the capacity. Waiting does not help. The last case is a configuration problem, not a traffic problem: Read $e->reason() for the registry code and $e->decision->retryAfter for the wait when there is one. See Error handling for what to do with each. A fallback for refusals is a choice By default a fallback replaces only what your callable did. A rate-limit refusal passes through to you. You can let the fallback replace refusals too, but a fallback that hides refusals also hides that you are being throttled. See Fallback . Testing a limit A test of a rate limit that refills over a minute should not wait a minute. Move a virtual clock instead: see Testing with the virtual clock . Previous Retry and budget Next Circuit breaker"},{"t":"Circuit breaker","u":"guides/circuit-breaker.html","d":"How the circuit breaker judges a window of completed calls, opens, probes and closes again, what counts as a failure, and what a refusal looks like.","h":[["what-a-circuit-breaker-is-for","What a circuit breaker is for"],["configure-it","Configure it"],["read-the-arguments-in-order","Read the arguments in order"],["closed-open-half-open","Closed, open, half-open"],["what-counts-as-a-failure","What counts as a failure"],["testing-recovery","Testing recovery"]],"x":"Alpha Circuit state lives in one PHP process. Under PHP-FPM a pipeline built inside a request starts every request with a closed circuit, and no request sees another's failures. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from examples/09-choosing-policies.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. The numbers make behavior visible. They are not recommendations. What a circuit breaker is for A dependency is down and callers keep hitting it. Every call waits, fails and adds load. The breaker watches the outcomes of recent calls. When too many failed, it opens and refuses new calls at once, without running your code. After a while it lets a few probe calls through to see whether the dependency has recovered. Configure it The snippet below opens a circuit after repeated failures and shows what the fifth call gets. It also builds the same breaker over a time window. Shared setup for the snippets on this page While the circuit is closed, your own exception comes through unchanged (the empty catch above). Once it is open, the call is refused with AdmissionRejectedException and reason circuit_open , and the callable runs no more: four invocations in total. A refused call never reaches your callable, and the refusal is not a sample. Read the arguments in order circuitBreaker(window, minimumSamples, failureThreshold, openDuration, probeCapacity, probeSuccessThreshold, permitLease) : Argument Meaning window lastCalls(n) keeps the last n completed non-neutral outcomes. timeWindow(window, bucketGranularity) aggregates outcomes in buckets and is approximate at its boundary. minimumSamples How many outcomes must exist before the circuit may open. It cannot exceed a count window. failureThreshold The failure share at which it opens, as a Ratio , for example Ratio::of(1, 2) for half. openDuration How long it stays open before it admits probes. probeCapacity How many probe calls may be in flight when it is half-open. probeSuccessThreshold How many successful probes close it. It cannot exceed the probe capacity. permitLease How long a call's permit stays valid. It must exceed the attempt timeout by more than 1 second. Closed, open, half-open Closed. Calls pass, and each completed outcome is recorded in the window. When the window holds at least minimumSamples and the share of failures reaches the threshold, the circuit opens. Open. Calls are refused with circuit_open . After openDuration the circuit becomes half-open. Half-open. Up to probeCapacity probes are admitted. It closes after probeSuccessThreshold successful probes. A failed probe reopens it at once, and a refused call ( circuit_probe_capacity_exhausted ) is not a sample. The circuit is safe against stale or lost results. A probe that is abandoned or expires frees its slot without counting as success or failure, and a result that arrives after the circuit reopened cannot change its state. Every permit ends exactly once: on success, on exception and on cancellation. The circuit emits a CircuitTransitioned event on every change ( API reference ). What counts as a failure Your classifier decides. With ExceptionClassifier : every exception counts as a circuit failure, unless you list its class in ignoreForCircuit() , for example your own validation error, which says nothing about the dependency; once you give a countAsCircuitFailure() list, only the listed classes (and retryable ones) are failures, and every other exception is circuit-neutral; unacceptableValuesWhen() marks a returned value as a failure, for example an error payload returned with a normal status; refusals by other protections, cancellations and storage errors are neutral: they neither open nor heal the circuit. A call that settles after the operation deadline is neutral too: the caller already got DeadlineExceeded , and the late result says nothing about the dependency that the circuit may act on. Give the callable its own timeout shorter than the pipeline deadline, so that a slow dependency fails inside the deadline and counts. Slow-call counting, as an option, is planned for a later release. An exception nobody listed counts as a failure, because a circuit that ignored unknown failures would stay closed against a dependency that fails in a way nobody anticipated. Testing recovery A test of \"open, wait 30 seconds, probe, close\" should not wait 30 seconds. Move a virtual clock: see Testing with the virtual clock . Previous Rate limiting Next Bulkhead and cooldown"},{"t":"Bulkhead and cooldown","u":"guides/bulkhead-and-cooldown.html","d":"Limit the work in flight with a leased bulkhead, and pause a whole partition after a server-stated wait with a shared cooldown. What each refuses and what a lease does not do.","h":[["bulkhead","Bulkhead"],["cooldown","Cooldown"],["the-order-of-checks","The order of checks"],["testing-both","Testing both"]],"x":"Alpha Both protections keep their state in one PHP process. The bulkhead counts the operations in flight in that process only, and a cooldown pauses new operations in that process only. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from examples/09-choosing-policies.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. Bulkhead One slow dependency can use up all the concurrent work a process can do, and then everything else waits behind it. A bulkhead limits operations in flight at once, so the rest of the process keeps working. Shared setup for the snippets on this page The snippet allows one operation in flight. While the outer operation holds the only slot, an inner call is refused: bulkhead(capacity, lease) : capacity is the number of slots. A slot is held while an attempt runs and released when it settles. Nothing is held during a retry delay. lease bounds how long a slot stays reserved if its owner vanishes. It must exceed the attempt timeout by more than 1 second. The refusal is an AdmissionRejectedException with reason concurrency_capacity_exhausted . Your callable did not run. What a lease does not do A lease expiring does not stop a callback that is still running. The slot becomes free again, so a new operation may start while the first callback is still running, and the number of callbacks actually running can briefly exceed the capacity. A late release cannot free a newer holder's slot, and every permit ends exactly once. Reqtide cannot stop your code. Make the callable respect the attempt timeout. Cooldown When a server answers \"slow down, retry after 60 seconds\", every other operation in the same partition should wait too, not find out one by one. A cooldown is that shared pause. cooldown(cleanupGrace) turns it on. cleanupGrace is how long the state is kept after the pause ended. The cooldown activates only when your classifier reports waits, with ExceptionClassifier::retryAfterFrom() . Without one it never activates. Reqtide has no HTTP awareness, so turning a 429 and its header into a Duration is your job. The snippet below continues the pipeline from Retry and budget , where the first operation was deferred because a 60-second wait does not fit the retry policy's 100 ms maximum delay. The second operation never calls the callable: A positive Retry-After extends the shared cooldown for the partition. While it is active, new operations are deferred with cooldown_active , or with cooldown_exceeds_deadline when the wait does not fit the operation's own deadline, as above. The callable ran once in total. The order of checks The cooldown is checked after the circuit and the bulkhead and before the rate limit. If a later check refuses, the permits taken earlier are released. See Concepts . Testing both A bulkhead and a cooldown depend on time. Test them with a virtual clock instead of waiting: see Testing with the virtual clock . Previous Circuit breaker Next Fallback"},{"t":"Fallback","u":"guides/fallback.html","d":"Attach a fallback to one operation, choose whether it may also replace refusals by the rate limit, circuit breaker, bulkhead or cooldown, and know what it never replaces.","h":[["a-fallback-belongs-to-one-operation","A fallback belongs to one operation"],["choose-what-it-may-replace","Choose what it may replace"],["what-a-fallback-never-replaces","What a fallback never replaces"],["when-the-fallback-itself-fails","When the fallback itself fails"],["testing-a-fallback","Testing a fallback"]],"x":"Alpha Names and behavior may change before a first release. State is process-local. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from examples/09-choosing-policies.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. The longer annotated script is examples/04-fallback.php . A fallback belongs to one operation A fallback is a FallbackOperation you attach to a single operation, through the fallback: argument of newContext() . It receives the original Outcome and returns the replacement value. A pipeline with no fallback on the context never replaces anything. Two things must both be true before a fallback replaces a failure: the operation carries a fallback, and your classifier marked the exception fallback-eligible with ExceptionClassifier::fallbackOn() . The default is none. An exception you did not list is returned to you. Choose what it may replace By default a fallback may only replace what your callable did. With FallbackScope::IncludeAdmissionRefusals it may also replace a circuit, bulkhead, rate or cooldown refusal. This snippet spends the only rate-limit token and then makes a second call under each scope: Shared setup for the snippets on this page The default scope lets the refusal through, so you still know you are being throttled. With IncludeAdmissionRefusals the second call returns the cached value, and the refusal is not lost: detailed mode keeps it as policyCause . Choose the second scope deliberately. A fallback that hides refusals also hides that you are being throttled. fallbackScope(IncludeAdmissionRefusals) needs at least one admission policy (a rate limit, circuit breaker, bulkhead or cooldown); the builder rejects the combination otherwise. What a fallback never replaces A deadline. If the total deadline passed, the result is deadline_exceeded . A cancellation. An infrastructure failure, such as an unreachable state backend. Reqtide does not invent behavior when its own state is down. A fallback shares the operation's total deadline: it runs inside the same budget as the attempts before it. When the fallback itself fails A fallback that throws is reported as FallbackFailedException in simple mode, with the fallback's exception as getPrevious() , and as the disposition fallback_failed in detailed mode . The original failure is retained on the exception. The annotated script 04-fallback.php shows both. Testing a fallback Script the failing attempts and assert on the dispatch count and the recorded events: see Testing with the virtual clock . Previous Bulkhead and cooldown Next Error handling"},{"t":"Error handling","u":"guides/error-handling.html","d":"Tell your own failures from Reqtide decisions, branch on ReasonCode and Problem, and read the full account of an operation in detailed mode.","h":[["two-kinds-of-failure","Two kinds of failure"],["step-1-set-up-a-pipeline-that-refuses","Step 1: Set up a pipeline that refuses"],["step-2-catch-a-refusal-and-read-its-reason","Step 2: Catch a refusal and read its reason"],["step-3-your-own-exceptions-pass-through","Step 3: Your own exceptions pass through"],["step-4-detailed-mode-returns-the-same-facts-as-data","Step 4: Detailed mode returns the same facts as data"],["step-5-when-the-retry-policy-gives-up","Step 5: When the retry policy gives up"],["step-6-configuration-mistakes-name-the-option","Step 6: Configuration mistakes name the option"],["using-the-registry","Using the registry"],["common-mistakes","Common mistakes"]],"x":"Alpha Names and reason codes may still change before a first release. The registry's append-only policy (below) applies from the first release on. Every code block comes from examples/07-error-handling.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. Concepts first, then the code. Two kinds of failure Kind What you receive Example Your failure Your own exception, unchanged, with its own class and message. Reqtide never wraps it. The upstream timed out A Reqtide decision A typed exception that implements ProblemCarrier (and ReqtideException ) The rate limit refused the call; the circuit is open; the deadline passed A third group is a configuration mistake: InvalidConfigurationException (also a ProblemCarrier ), raised before anything is dispatched. Step 1: Set up a pipeline that refuses The pipeline admits one call per hour, so the second call is refused. The first call goes through. Output so far: Step 2: Catch a refusal and read its reason Output: reason() returns a ReasonCode . Branch on it, never on getMessage() . problem() returns a Problem : a redacted, JSON-serializable report shaped after RFC 9457 ( type , title , detail ) with Reqtide members ( category , code , stage , severity , retryability , ids). Because the exception came out of execute() , it names the operation ( operationId , profileVersion , correlationId ). A Problem is built from code metadata only. Exception messages, request data and secrets never enter it; an underlying cause appears as a class name and a code. The member names and their order are API. title and detail wording is not: do not parse it. Ignore unknown members. Retryability Value Meaning after_wait A policy refused the call; try again after a wait transient Retrying later may succeed and no side-effect uncertainty exists never Fix the configuration or the code; repeating the same call cannot help unknown A mutation may have been applied; reconcile instead of repeating blindly These describe the Reqtide decision, not your business result. Categories and severity category() is one of policy , infrastructure , configuration , classification , fallback , admission , retry , accounting , preparation . severity() is advisory and uses the PSR-3 level names ( debug , info , notice , warning , error , critical ) as plain strings; Core has no PSR-3 dependency, and no registry code currently uses critical . The refusals shown here are info : a refusal is the protection working. Step 3: Your own exceptions pass through Output: Step 4: Detailed mode returns the same facts as data executeDetailed() returns a refusal as data in an ExecutionCompletion instead of throwing it. Output: The dispositions are succeeded , failed , rejected , deferred , cancelled , deadline_exceeded , fallback_succeeded and fallback_failed . The completion also carries the per-attempt records, bounded diagnostics, and a summary of counts ( dispatched , retries , admittedNotDispatched , fallbackExecutions and more). problem() is null when there is nothing for Reqtide to report, for example after a success or a plain business failure. Step 5: When the retry policy gives up When retries stop because of the retry policy itself, your last exception is kept as the result, and the reason is recorded as a retry -stage problem. A refusal by another protection (circuit, bulkhead, rate limit, cooldown) on a later attempt behaves differently: the refusal becomes the result, and your last exception is attached as getPrevious() . Output: The simple execute() call would throw your UpstreamTimeout here; detailed mode explains why it stopped ( attempts_exhausted ). Step 6: Configuration mistakes name the option Output: Using the registry Branch on $exception->reason() , $problem->code , an event's reason() or a diagnostic's reason() . Keep a default branch when you match over ReasonCode : new cases are added in minor releases, so an exhaustive match over it is unsupported. (The match in step 2 is over Retryability .) Append-only policy: a published value never changes meaning and is never reused; changing a code's category or retryability is breaking; severity and wording are advisory. A retired code stays emitted for at least one minor release. Codes from foreign components (your classifier's own outcome codes, a third-party backend's reasons) are not registry members; ReasonCode::tryFrom() returns null for them. The full registry is the ReasonCode enum in src/Error/ReasonCode.php . Common mistakes Catching \\Throwable and treating every exception as an upstream failure. A refusal is not your callable failing: when the pipeline refuses, your callable was not invoked. Retrying a rate.exhausted refusal immediately in a loop. It says after_wait . Logging $e->getMessage() of a Reqtide exception as a stable identifier. Log reason()->value and problem()->type() instead. Previous Fallback Next Testing with the virtual clock"},{"t":"Testing with the virtual clock","u":"guides/testing.html","d":"Test retries, backoff, deadlines and circuits without sleeping: a virtual clock, scripted attempts and recorded events.","h":[["what-the-helpers-give-you","What the helpers give you"],["step-1-wire-one-virtual-clock-through-everything","Step 1: Wire one virtual clock through everything"],["step-2-anchor-the-context-on-the-same-clock","Step 2: Anchor the context on the same clock"],["step-3-test-backoff-without-sleeping","Step 3: Test backoff without sleeping"],["step-4-test-circuit-recovery-by-advancing-time","Step 4: Test circuit recovery by advancing time"],["tips","Tips"],["limits","Limits"]],"x":"Alpha The helpers ship inside the single enaxon/reqtide package, in the Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing namespace ( src/Testing ). enaxon/reqtide is not on Packagist yet, so install it as described in Install . The helpers are for tests: never call them from production code. Every class is plain (no base test case) and marked @api-for-tests . Every code block comes from examples/08-testing.php , which tools/run-examples.sh runs and compares with the recorded output. The example uses a small expect() function so it runs without a test framework; in a real suite use your framework's assertions. What the helpers give you Class Purpose VirtualClock Clock that moves only through advance() , advanceMonotonicOnly() , setMonotonic() (never backwards) and setWall() . Wall jumps are independent of the monotonic domain. failNextMonotonicReads(n) / failNextWallReads(n) inject ClockFailureException ; read counts are observable. SeededRandomSource RandomSource over Random\\Randomizer with an Mt19937 seed; same seed, same sequence. ScriptedRandomSource RandomSource returning a queued list of integers; records requested ranges and fails loudly when exhausted or out of range. VirtualDelayScheduler DelayScheduler ( BoundedBlocking ) that advances a VirtualClock instead of sleeping. Same checkpoint order as BoundedBlockingScheduler (deadline, then cancellation, then completion; equality is expired). cancelDuringNextDelay($token, $after) cancels mid-delay. Records every delay as a DelayRecord . RecordingEventSink EventSink storing events ( events() , ofType() ); throwOnEmit($e, $times) throws after recording, for failure-isolation tests. ControlledRuntimeAdapter Synchronous RuntimeAdapter driven by DispatchScript s: return a value, throw, call the real operation, advance the virtual clock during the call, request cancellation during the call. Records dispatchCount() , attemptContexts() and dispatches() so tests assert observed dispatches. DispatchScript Immutable per-dispatch behaviour ( returning , throwing , callingOperation , advancing , requestingCancellation ). ControlledExecution The settled RuntimeExecution the adapter returns. Observation: observed >= deadline is late ( DeadlineExceeded , evaluated before cancellation); otherwise requested cancellation gives Cancelled ; an unreadable clock gives null timing, incomplete accounting and a diagnostic. The actual outcome is always retained. RecordingAttemptOperation / RecordingPreparedAttempt AttemptOperation counting prepare calls, rewinds() (prepares after the first), replay verifications, invocations and discarded outcomes; one-shot injected preparation and replay failures. DispatchRecord , DelayRecord Read-only records exposed by the adapter and the scheduler. Use one VirtualClock instance for the Deadline , the scheduler and the adapter; a Deadline rejects any other clock. Step 1: Wire one virtual clock through everything Use one VirtualClock for the scheduler, the runtime adapter and the memory backends, so time only moves when you say so. InMemoryEnvironment (what compileInMemory() uses) wires the real clock and a scheduler that really sleeps, so a test builds the RuntimeEnvironment by hand as above. Never pair a VirtualClock with BoundedBlockingScheduler : it would wait for time that never passes. Step 2: Anchor the context on the same clock newContext() builds the deadline on the pipeline's own clock, so that part is automatic. Anything anchored to wall time, such as the replay-safety contract, must come from the same clock: a contract made from the real clock lies in the virtual clock's past or future and is treated as not valid (the retry is then denied and replay_contract_not_valid is recorded). A VirtualClock starts at a fixed date ( 2026-01-01T00:00:00+00:00 UTC) rather than the real time, so a test gives the same answer on every day; startingAt() picks another start. Step 3: Test backoff without sleeping The first two scripted dispatches throw the retryable error, so the pipeline waits 100 ms and then 200 ms (exponential backoff, no jitter) and the third dispatch succeeds. The wait happened on the virtual clock: nothing slept. Step 4: Test circuit recovery by advancing time Output of the whole script: Tips Seed randomness ( SeededRandomSource ) when you test jitter, and compare two runs with the same seed; assert the bound (full jitter stays between zero and the computed delay), not a hard-coded draw. Assert on observed dispatches ( dispatchCount() ), not on what you think the pipeline did. A refused call must show zero dispatches. Give each test a fresh pipeline. State lives in the backends, so a reused pipeline carries quota, budget and circuit state from one test into the next. Limits A test that passes on virtual time shows that the policy logic does what you configured. It is not evidence about real timing, real transports or shared state. The helpers simulate settlement and delays; they do not model callback interruption or a physical transport stop. Previous Error handling Next Shared state with Redis"},{"t":"Shared state with Redis","u":"guides/redis.html","d":"Share one rate limit, circuit breaker, bulkhead, retry budget and cooldown between PHP processes with the Redis package. Set-up, ACL users, TLS, what each failure means, maintenance, and what a restart loses.","h":[["when-you-need-shared-state","When you need shared state"],["install","Install"],["set-up","Set up in four steps"],["a-runnable-example","A runnable example"],["server-settings","Server settings"],["tls","TLS"],["when-something-goes-wrong","When something goes wrong"],["maintenance","Maintenance"],["operator-actions","Operator actions"],["what-is-lost","What is lost on a restart or an eviction"],["limits","Limits"],["run-the-failure-demo","Run the failure demo with Redis down"],["related","Related"]],"x":"Alpha The package enaxon/reqtide-redis is not released and not on Packagist. It works in the development repository and was run only in local Docker, on the command line. PHP-FPM, Sentinel, Redis Cluster, Valkey and replicas are not supported. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from packages/redis/examples . tools/run-redis-examples.sh runs those scripts against a local Redis and compares their output with the recorded output shown here. When you need shared state The core package keeps its state in the memory of one PHP process. A rate limit there protects that process only. Ten workers each get the full limit, and each has its own circuit breaker. The Redis package moves the state of five policies into one Redis server: the rate limit, the circuit breaker, the bulkhead, the retry budget and the cooldown. Every process that uses the same registry draws from the same quota and sees the same open circuit. You need it when Several workers or servers call the same API, and the API limit applies to all of them together. One slow dependency should open the circuit for every process at once. A server asked you to wait, and every process should wait. You do not need it when One long-lived process makes all the calls: a single worker, a command line job or a daemon. The in-memory pipeline is enough. Losing the protection when Redis loses its data is not acceptable to you. This package does not survive data loss. See What is lost . Shared state costs a network call to Redis for each policy step of each protected call. It also adds a new failure: Redis can be down. When it is, the protected call does not run. See When something goes wrong . Install Not on Packagist composer require enaxon/reqtide-redis does not work yet. There is no tagged release. Use Composer path repositories, as in Install . Register the clone once for the core package and once for the folder of this package. symlink: false makes Composer copy the packages into vendor/ : The vendor name is enaxon . Copy the package name exactly. The package needs PHP 8.3 or newer, the openssl extension and predis/predis 3.7 or newer in the 3.x line. It requires the same version of enaxon/reqtide , so the core package comes from the first repository. Name the core package too: the adapter requires the same dev version of it, and a project with the default minimum stability refuses it otherwise. Checked on 2026-10-11 with Composer 2 on PHP 8.3 in Docker, from a clone, with these path repositories. Set up in four steps The host does steps 1, 2 and 4. The application does step 3. Step 1: Create the registry A registry is the unit of isolation: a key prefix, an identity and two ceilings. Create one for each deployment. The application never creates it. If the registry is missing, every protected call is rejected and nothing creates it for you. The first argument is the key prefix. Every key of the registry starts with it. The second is the identity of the registry: 1 to 64 letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens. initialize() refuses a registry that exists with a different identity or different ceilings. The third is a secret of 32 bytes or more. It is used to hash tenant and subject names inside keys. Keep it for the life of the registry, because a new secret changes every hashed key. Do not use a value that is also a credential elsewhere. The example uses random_bytes(32) because its registry is thrown away at the end. The two optional ceilings are maxPartitions (default 10,000) and maxRecords (default 20,000). Each is between 1 and 1,000,000. See Limits . initialize() can be called again with the same values. connection() and check() are helpers of the example, in support.php . connection() builds the connection options from the environment of the example. In your project you build RedisConnectionOptions yourself, with the administration user (see Server settings and TLS ). Step 2: Check the server and create the users inspectServer() reads maxmemory-policy and maxmemory . protectsState() returns true when the policy is noeviction or the server has no memory limit. It returns false when the server may delete keys, and null when the policy could not be read, for example because the user may not run CONFIG GET . The example refuses to go on unless the answer is true. See Server settings for why. Then give the application a Redis user with only the rules in RedisAcl::execution($registry) , and the host a user with RedisAcl::administration($registry) . Print the exact tokens from your registry: echo implode(' ', RedisAcl::execution($registry)); The result has this shape. The list of commands is short and it comes from the library, so print it instead of copying it from this page: The user may run the shipped scripts and the commands they use, only on keys under the registry prefix. The administration user has the same rules and four more commands: SCAN and UNLINK for the purge of a retired registry, CONFIG GET for inspectServer() and INFO for the check of the server version. The tests create users from these rules, run every operation, and remove one rule at a time. Each rule is needed. With one rule missing, the first call fails as unavailable and writes nothing. Step 3: Build the pipeline Build the pipeline on the Redis environment instead of compileInMemory() . Creating the environment opens no connection. Nothing is read or written until the first operation. A backend exists only for the policies the definition configures: You pass the classifier yourself, as with compileInMemory() . The clock of every decision is the clock of the Redis server, so the clocks of your PHP hosts do not matter for the state. The connection options take the host, the connect timeout and the read and write timeout in seconds, then the port, the user and the password. Both timeouts are required, greater than zero and at most 3,600 seconds. A user name needs a password. The password never shows in var_dump and never appears in an exception message. The options refuse serialization. RedisEnvironment::explain() describes the compiled pipeline and its shared state. It makes no network call. It shows the state scope, whose clock decides, the I/O timeout, TLS, the registry identity and its ceilings. It never prints the host, the password, the key prefix or the secret: Step 4: Run maintenance Run (new RedisRegistryMaintenance($connection, $registry))->step() from a worker or a scheduler, and call it again while the result says more . Nothing runs it for you. See Maintenance . A runnable example The example builds two pipelines separately. Each has its own Redis connection, like two PHP processes. They share one registry and draw from one token bucket of three tokens. Whoever asks, only three calls are served. Imports for the snippets on this page The first part of the script is in steps 1 to 3. The rest builds both pipelines, makes five calls and removes the registry: From the development repository, this starts Redis 7.4.11 in Docker, runs the three example scripts and compares their output with the recorded files: The output is: Calls 1 to 3 are served, two of them through pipeline A and one through B. Calls 4 and 5 are refused with the problem code rate.exhausted , and their callable never runs. The pipelines share nothing in PHP. The quota lives in Redis. Server settings Minimum version: Redis 7.0. The project tests Redis 7.4.11 and 8.2.10. The scripts use redis.acl_check_cmd , which Redis 7.0 added. initialize() and inspectServer() refuse an older server with InvalidConfigurationException , and every script fails before its first command on one. Set maxmemory-policy noeviction . With a policy that evicts keys, the server can delete a partition without any signal, and its protection starts again from nothing. With noeviction , a full server refuses writes and the call ends with a clear failure instead. Only the loss of the registry itself is reported. The state lives in Redis memory. See What is lost for restarts. Standalone server, RESP2, one direct connection. There is no retry option and no persistent connection. A command is never sent twice. Scripts are sent by digest. The text is loaded with SCRIPT LOAD only when the server has lost its script cache. TLS Set tls: true in the connection options. The server certificate and the host name are always checked, and there is no option to turn that off. Two more options exist: caFile is the path of a CA file, for a private CA. The file must exist, or the options throw InvalidConfigurationException . tlsServerName is the name to check when you connect by address. A caFile or tlsServerName without tls: true is refused. An example is new RedisConnectionOptions('redis.internal', 1.0, 0.5, 6379, 'app', $password, tls: true, caFile: '/etc/ssl/redis-ca.pem') . TLS was tested locally against servers with a per-run test CA. Refused TLS, a wrong password and a missing ACL rule all end as StateUnavailableException . When something goes wrong The library never answers a failed state call with fresh local state. It never sends a command twice. It maps each failure to one of Core's infrastructure exceptions, and the protected callable is not run unless the table says otherwise. What happened What you get What was written Server unreachable, TLS refused, wrong password, a user without a needed rule, or a server that is full ( noeviction ) StateUnavailableException . The callable is not run Nothing The connection was lost or timed out after a command was sent IndeterminateStateException for a write, StateUnavailableException for a read. The library never sends the command again Unknown for a write: it may have been applied once The registry is missing StateUnavailableException Nothing. The registry is not created Stored data is damaged CorruptStateException Nothing of the call. Cleanup of expired state that ran before the damage was found stays, and the registry counters stay in step with it A key under the prefix holds a value of the wrong type CorruptStateException Nothing of the call The registry ceiling is reached StateCapacityExceededException , after expired state was reclaimed and the oldest finished records were evicted Nothing What happens to your operation after an uncertain result depends on the step: After an uncertain debit of the rate limit or the bulkhead, the operation is not run. After an uncertain release or finalization, the result of your call is returned. The accounting is incomplete. After an uncertain retry commit, no retry is sent. Whether to run the work without protection when the state is down is your decision. Make it in your own catch block. The library does not make it for you. Error handling shows how to tell these exceptions from the failures of your own code. Maintenance RedisRegistryMaintenance::step() removes state that no longer protects anything. Nothing calls it for you. One step looks at no more than 128 finished records and no more than 128 candidates in one atomic script call. It removes: finished records (finalization tombstones, finished budget and circuit records) after a retention of 120 seconds; rate limit and retry budget partitions that are equal to fresh state after 120 seconds without a write; expired leases of bulkhead partitions, and the partition when nothing is left in it; cooldowns whose end and cleanup grace have passed; closed circuits without history, permits or finished records after 120 seconds without a write. It never touches an unexpired lease, a pending reservation, a retry budget balance, an open or half-open circuit, the count history of a closed circuit, or a cooldown that is still in force. The queue head and the order of the index are the saved position, so each step continues where the last one stopped, on any machine and after a restart. A step that reports more found a full batch: call it again until more is false. One step never scans the whole registry. It writes no event and changes no decision, and it does not create a missing registry. Operator actions The host runs these with its own connection. The library never calls them. Action Call Close a circuit and make its permits stale RedisCircuitAdmin::reset($policyIdentity) Retire a registry RedisRegistryAdmin::retire() , then purgeRetired() to remove what it left Check the registry without writing RedisRegistryAdmin::verify() Check the server policy RedisRegistryAdmin::inspectServer() (needs CONFIG GET and INFO ) reset() closes the circuit, discards its history and every active permit, and moves it to a new generation. It returns the new generation, or null when the partition does not exist. A lost reply is an indeterminate result, and the reset is never repeated. The identity is the one of the circuit policy, for example $builder->definition()->circuit->identity . retire() deletes the registry metadata. Partition and record keys stay, and the returned status shows how many. purgeRetired() removes them, in pages, and returns how many keys it removed. It refuses to run while the registry exists. Run it again after an interruption. initialize() refuses a prefix that still holds the index or the tombstone queue of a retired registry. Purge first. What is lost on a restart or an eviction The state lives in Redis memory. A restart without persistence, a flush or a failover loses it: quota balances, open circuits, leases and budgets. A lost registry rejects every protected call with StateUnavailableException until you initialize it again. No fresh state is ever used in place of lost state. A lost partition inside a surviving registry is not reported. Protection of that partition starts again from nothing. This happens when the server evicts keys, which is why you set noeviction . The registry counters and the saved cleanup position are part of the state, so they are lost with it. Protection that survives data loss on restart or failover is not planned for the first release. Limits Limit What it means Standalone server only One Redis server, one direct connection. Sentinel, replicas and Valkey were not tested and are not supported. Redis Cluster is not supported by design The scripts also touch keys they derive themselves: members of the registry index and entries of the tombstone queue, beyond the keys passed to them. The ACL pattern ~prefix:* covers those keys. Redis 7.0 or newer Older servers are refused. Tested on 7.4.11 and 8.2.10. PHP-FPM Not run, no claim. Cost of one call A script call is atomic and blocks the server while it runs. Most calls touch a few keys. Two paths cost time in proportion to the records of one partition: a circuit that changes state removes every permit it still holds, and a bulkhead partition removes the leases that expired. maxRecords Bounds those two paths. It can be up to 1,000,000. Keep it as low as the load needs. Only five policies Rate limit, circuit breaker, bulkhead, retry budget and cooldown. The deadline, the retry delays and the attempt timeout are local to each process. Run the failure demo with Redis down The second example needs no server. It points at a port where nothing listens. The state cannot be read, so Reqtide cannot decide whether the call is allowed. The callable is not run, and you get a typed exception: The connection options point at port 1 of the local host, with timeouts of half a second. The pipeline has a rate limit, so every call needs the state: A state failure is an infrastructure exception. It is a different thing from a refusal by a policy. The callable ran 0 times. Nothing was written, because the failure happened before any command was sent. executeDetailed() reports the disposition failed and never succeeded . Related Rate limiting , Circuit breaker and Bulkhead and cooldown describe the policies this package shares. Guzzle requests with Reqtide shows how to pass these backends to ReqtideClient::withBackends() . API reference lists the classes of this package. Previous Testing with the virtual clock Next Guzzle requests with Reqtide"},{"t":"Guzzle requests with Reqtide","u":"guides/guzzle.html","d":"Wrap Guzzle requests with retries, deadlines, rate limits and circuit breaking. You state what may be sent twice. Body replay, Retry-After, the shared cooldown, client defaults, connection reuse, promises and limits.","h":[["install","Install"],["wrap-a-call","Wrap a call"],["what-may-be-sent-twice","What may be sent twice"],["retryable-outcomes","Retryable outcomes"],["bodies","Request bodies and replay"],["responses","Responses, failures, redirects and timeouts"],["retry-after","Retry-After and the shared cooldown"],["client-defaults","Client defaults"],["connections","Connection reuse"],["promises","Promises"],["secrets","Secrets and what events carry"],["uri","uri() and the base URI"],["limits","Limits and cost"],["related","Related"]],"x":"Alpha The package enaxon/reqtide-guzzle is not released and not on Packagist. It works in the development repository and was run only in local Docker, on the command line, with the cURL handlers. PHP-FPM, Guzzle 8, the stream handler and PSR-18 are not supported. See Limits and non-guarantees . Code blocks come from packages/guzzle/examples . tools/run-guzzle-examples.sh runs those scripts and compares their output with the recorded output shown here. The scripts start a small fixture server on the loopback interface and print what the server received. In your code the request goes to your API. Install Not on Packagist composer require enaxon/reqtide-guzzle does not work yet. There is no tagged release. Use Composer path repositories, as in Install . Register the clone once for the core package and once for the folder of this package: The package needs PHP 8.3 or newer, the curl extension and guzzlehttp/guzzle 7.15.5 or newer in the 7.x line. It requires the same version of enaxon/reqtide . Name the core package too: the adapter requires the same dev version of it, and a project with the default minimum stability refuses it otherwise. Checked on 2026-10-11 with Composer 2 on PHP 8.3 in Docker, from a clone, with these path repositories. Wrap a call ReqtideClient is the entry point. It owns its cURL handlers and runs each logical operation, with all its attempts, through one compiled Core pipeline. You build the pipeline with PipelineBuilder , as in Getting started , and pass the builder to the client. ReqtideClient::inMemory($builder, $configuration) keeps the state in this process. Under PHP-FPM every request would start with fresh state. ReqtideClient::withBackends($builder, rateLimit: ..., circuit: ..., ...) takes the state backends you give it, for example the Redis backends from Shared state with Redis . Pass a backend for every policy the pipeline configures. The clock of the decisions is the clock of the backend. Both take an optional GuzzleConfiguration and an optional EventSink . Each call takes an HttpOperation and a timeout for the whole operation. The timeout covers every attempt and every wait between attempts. Three methods run it: send() returns the response, or throws the failure of the operation. sendDetailed() returns an ExecutionCompletion with the summary of attempts and decisions instead of throwing. sendAsync() returns a promise. See Promises . Imports for the snippets on this page The first example sends a GET to a healthy server and to a server that always answers 503. The pipeline allows three attempts, 20 ms apart, with a retry budget: The numbers in the output are the server's own count of the requests it received: The 503 is not an exception. After the third attempt you get the last response back. sendDetailed() shows 3 dispatched attempts and 2 retries. attemptTimeout() is declared as TransportEnforced because the cURL timeouts enforce it. What may be sent twice Reqtide does not guess from the method or from a header. Each operation states whether it may be sent again. Three constructors on HttpOperation make that statement: Constructor Use it for What you state HttpOperation::read() GET and HEAD Repeating the request has no side effect (an RFC 9110 safe method), for the time you give as validFor . HttpOperation::idempotentMutation() POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE The server deduplicates the request by an identity, such as an Idempotency-Key . You give the identity, what it is unique within, and how the server treats a repeat. HttpOperation::single() Everything else Nothing. The request is sent once and never repeated. validFor is how long your statement holds, counted from now. It must be at least as long as the deadline you pass to send() . For a mutation, the identity is checked before each attempt, and the body is checked too: its SHA-256 digest must equal the digest of the first attempt, so one identity never carries two payloads. See Request bodies and replay . The default header is Idempotency-Key . Pass identityHeader: null when the identity travels elsewhere, and then you guarantee it stays the same. A header name has at most 64 letters, digits and hyphens. An identity value must not start or end with a space or a tab, because Guzzle trims header values. Core refuses a single() operation in a pipeline that retries more than once. The refusal comes before anything is sent. The second example sends a POST that the server answers with 503 each time. It checks the server's own log: The mutation was sent three times with the same body and the same key. The single() operation was sent once in a pipeline that allows one attempt, and it was refused with InvalidConfigurationException in the pipeline that retries. Your statement is the safety If you call read() for a request that does change something, or idempotentMutation() for a server that does not deduplicate, Reqtide sends it again. It cannot check the claim. The server must make the repeat harmless. Retryable outcomes A retry needs the three things described in Retry and budget : your statement that repeating is safe, a retry budget, and a classifier that marks the outcome as eligible. Here the statement is the operation you build, the budget is retryBudget() on the pipeline, and the classifier is HttpOutcomeClassifier . HTTP adds one more check: the reason code of the outcome must be in the list you give to the operation. That list is the $retryableOutcomeCodes argument of read() and idempotentMutation() . An empty list never retries The default of $retryableOutcomeCodes is an empty list. With the default, a pipeline that retries never retries. It only reports the reason outcome_not_retryable . Pass HttpOutcomeClassifier::starter()-> retryableOutcomeCodes() or your own list. If you build your own classifier, take the codes from that classifier. HttpOutcomeClassifier::starter() is a documented starting set. It is not a recommendation for every API. HttpOutcomeClassifier::empty() retries and falls back on nothing and reads no Retry-After . Both return a new classifier from every with...() call. Nothing is inferred from the status class. These are the starter rules: Outcome Eligible for retry Counts for the circuit Reason code Status below 400 No Success http_status_<code> 408, 502, 503, 504 Yes Failure http_status_<code> 429 Yes Neutral (handled through Retry-After and the cooldown) http_status_429 500 No Failure http_status_500 Any other 4xx or 5xx No Success http_status_<code> Transport error 6, 7, 28, 52, 55 or 56 Yes Failure http_transport_errno_<n> TLS or certificate error No Failure http_transport_errno_<n> Malformed or unsupported request No Neutral http_transport_errno_<n> Any other transport error No Failure http_transport_errno_<n> A 4xx other than 408 and 429 shows that the server is alive and judged the request, so it is never retried and it closes a half-open circuit. A 500 is not retried by default because it often means a defect that repeats. Add it with withRetryableStatuses() if your API says otherwise. TLS and certificate errors and malformed-request errors can never be made retryable. No status is eligible for a fallback in the starter set. An exception that carries a response is classified exactly like the response. Request bodies and replay A request that may be sent again needs a body that can be sent again: A seekable body is rewound to position 0 and checked before each attempt. A body that cannot be rewound needs a body factory: a closure that returns a fresh stream of known size each time. Pass it as bodyFactory . Anything else is refused before the first request. The content of the body is protected as well. With the default BodyIntegrity::Verified , Reqtide reads the body through a SHA-256 digest before the first attempt, again before every later attempt and at the last check before dispatch. A body with another digest is refused before anything is sent. The body is never held in memory, but it is read one more time for each check, so a body must be seekable (a factory must return a seekable stream) and at most maxVerifiedBodyBytes long (16 MiB by default). Pass BodyIntegrity::CallerAsserted for a larger body or one that cannot be read twice. Then you guarantee that the bytes stay the same, and only the size and the start position are checked. The time spent reading a body is not part of the attempt timeout. A single() operation may have a body that cannot be rewound, because it is sent once. A request with a body of known size gets an empty Expect header, unless it already has one, so cURL does not wait for 100 Continue . Responses, failures, redirects and timeouts A 4xx or 5xx status is a response. After the last attempt you get the last response. http_errors is forced off for every transfer. A transport failure (refused connection, timeout) is the original Guzzle exception, unless a policy ends the operation first. Its message holds the full URL. It is your result and it is returned as it is. Policy refusals (rate limit, circuit, bulkhead, cooldown) are Reqtide exceptions. See Error handling . Redirects are not followed. A 3xx is a response, and its target is never requested. allow_redirects is forced off and cannot be set. Timeouts. The timeout of an attempt is the smaller of your attempt timeout and the time left in the operation. It goes to cURL as timeout and connect_timeout . read_timeout is never used, because the cURL handlers ignore it. Retry-After and the shared cooldown The classifier reads Retry-After for 429 and 503 responses. Change the list with withRetryAfterStatuses() . The value can be a number of seconds or an HTTP date (the IMF-fixdate form, or the older RFC 850 and asctime forms). A value that cannot be read, or one beyond the range of the shared-state duration (9,007,199,254 seconds), is dropped and gives no wait. It is never cut down or invented. With several header lines, the greatest valid delay wins. Clock skew between the server and your host remains a limit of date values. The wait is used in two ways. It is a lower bound for the next attempt of this operation: Reqtide never sends sooner. A wait that does not fit the maximum delay of the retry policy or the deadline defers the operation and returns the wait to you. With a cooldown() policy in the pipeline, a positive wait also starts a shared pause for the partition, so the next calls are deferred without a request. The third example shows this: The 429 carried Retry-After: 30 . The next call, with a deadline of 1 second, was deferred at once and the server's count stayed at 1. The reason is cooldown_exceeds_deadline because 30 seconds do not fit in the 1 second deadline. With a longer deadline the reason is cooldown_active . explain() sent nothing. The cooldown maximum A server can send a very long Retry-After , by fault or on purpose. The core cooldown policy caps the shared pause that one reported wait can start. The default cap is 3,600 seconds (one hour). A longer wait still governs the retry of the operation that received it, but the pause other operations see is cut to the cap. Set your own cap with the second argument of PipelineBuilder::cooldown($cleanupGrace, $maximumExtension) . See Bulkhead and cooldown . Client defaults GuzzleConfiguration::$clientDefaults accepts only these Guzzle options: headers , auth , proxy , verify , cert , ssl_key , crypto_method , force_ip_resolve , version and decode_content . headers only fill in headers that a request does not have, so they cannot change the identity header that is checked on each attempt. Any other option is refused with an InvalidConfigurationException that names it. Examples are delay , query , body , json , sink , curl , cookies , on_headers , on_stats , timeout , base_uri and handler . The reason: a client default applies to every transfer, outside the replay checks and outside the deadline. A default delay would sleep beyond the deadline. A default curl could replace the timeouts and the connection rules below. Put per-request values in the request. The client builds and owns its handlers: CurlHandler for send() and CurlMultiHandler for sendAsync() . You cannot pass a handler. Connection reuse libcurl can send a request again on a new connection, whatever its method, when a reused keep-alive connection gives no bytes back. Reqtide cannot see that second dispatch. So the rule is: A single() operation sets CURLOPT_FRESH_CONNECT and CURLOPT_FORBID_REUSE , and nothing can override it. The cost is a new TCP and TLS handshake for every such request. A read() and an idempotentMutation() may reuse connections, because a repeat of them is safe by your own statement. One risk stays: a retry that your own proxy or load balancer makes behind the connection is not visible here. Promises sendAsync() returns a promise that settles only after the whole operation has ended, finalization included. It is lazy. Guzzle has no event loop, so the operation runs when you call wait() , one operation at a time. While it waits, the driver runs the process-wide Guzzle promise task queue. Unrelated promise callbacks of your program also run inside the deadline loop. There is no claim of concurrent operations, of completion without a wait() , or of a guaranteed stop of a running transfer. Cancelling asks the transport to stop. It does not prove that it stopped. Pass a Cancellation to send() , sendDetailed() or sendAsync() . The synchronous and the promise runtime share the same policy state. Secrets and what events carry Events, summaries, diagnostics and problems carry descriptor fields only: the method, a fingerprint of the origin, your operation group, and a fingerprint of the Authorization and Proxy-Authorization headers of the request you give to the operation, keyed with the credentialKey you pass. ReqtideClient resolves the fingerprint against the client defaults, so it covers the credentials that are really sent: default headers for the headers the request lacks, a Basic auth default (which replaces the Authorization header), Digest auth and the user information of a proxy that applies to the request. If you run an operation through your own pipeline, call $operation-> forClient($configuration) before you read metadata() . They never carry a URL, a query, a header or a body. Random secrets were placed in all of those and searched for in everything Reqtide produces, and in Redis keys and values, with no hit. Two things are outside that promise: The exception that Guzzle throws for a transport failure holds the full URL. It is your result, and it is returned as it is. Stack traces can hold arguments. Set zend.exception_ignore_args=On in production. uri() and the base URI Give GuzzleConfiguration an absolute http or https baseUri . $client-> uri('/orders/42') resolves a reference against it as RFC 3986 says, and the request of an HttpOperation needs that absolute result. A malformed reference throws InvalidConfigurationException . The base URI is not a security boundary An absolute reference, or one that starts with // , replaces the base host. A reference orders against https://h/v1 gives https://h/orders . Reqtide does not check where a request goes. Keep requests to internal addresses out with your network egress rules. Limits and cost No cap on the response size. A hostile or faulty server can fill temporary storage within the timeout window. Use a sink or limits in your own layer. Process-wide task queue. A promise wait runs unrelated Guzzle callbacks. See Promises . Not supported: PSR-18, Guzzle 8, the stream handler, streaming bodies and PHP-FPM. Only the cURL handlers are used, and you cannot pass a handler. Shared state needs backends such as the Redis ones. inMemory() keeps limits in the process. Shared limits, circuit and cooldown across processes were tested with up to 6 processes. First request. The client loads about 100 to 150 more files than bare Guzzle. Use an optimized autoloader and OPcache in production. Cost These figures were measured on one laptop under Docker Desktop. The run-to-run noise was large. They are no claim about your system. A call through the client with in-memory state added about 30 to 130 microseconds to a bare Guzzle call with one policy set, and about 135 to 235 microseconds with five. The first request of a process took 14 ms with bare Guzzle and 26 to 35 ms with the client when the sources were on a local disk. With the sources on a slow bind mount, the first request took 0.6 to 1 second. Related Retry and budget explains the safety statement and the retry budget in Core. Shared state with Redis shows the backends for withBackends() . API reference lists the classes of this package. Previous Shared state with Redis Next API reference"},{"t":"API reference","u":"guides/api-reference.html","d":"The public surface you use day to day, with signatures generated from the real classes: PipelineBuilder, CompiledPipeline, ExceptionClassifier, policies, outcomes, events, Problem and the test helpers. The classes of the Redis and Guzzle packages have their own groups.","h":[["how-to-read-this-page","How to read this page"],["the-shortest-path-through-the-surface","The shortest path through the surface"],["build","Build a pipeline"],["run","Run an operation"],["classify","Decide what an outcome means"],["options","Policy options"],["results","Results of an operation"],["errors","Errors and reasons"],["events","Events"],["testing","Test helpers"],["redis-package","Redis package: set up shared state"],["redis-operate","Redis package: operate"],["redis-backends","Redis package: backends"],["guzzle-package","Guzzle package"]],"x":"Alpha Nothing is released and no version number exists. Any name or signature may change without notice. See Limits and non-guarantees . How to read this page The types of the core package live under the namespace Enaxon\\Reqtide ; each heading shows the namespace after the class name. The last four groups on this page list classes of two separate packages, enaxon/reqtide-redis (namespace Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis ) and enaxon/reqtide-guzzle (namespace Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle ). Those packages are not released and not part of the core archive. Listings show the public surface in source order: constructors, public properties, enum cases and public methods. Members marked @internal in the source are left out. Parameter types are shown without namespaces. Namespace What it holds Pipeline The builder, the compiled pipeline and the in-memory environment. Execution , Fallback Per-operation context, the safety statement and the fallback contract. Outcome Classifiers and the verdicts they return. Policy , Value Option enums and objects, Duration and Ratio . Completion The result of detailed execution. Error , Exception ReasonCode , Problem and the typed exceptions. Telemetry Typed events and the sink contract. Testing Test helpers, shipped in the same package. Redis Shared state on Redis. Separate package enaxon/reqtide-redis . See Shared state with Redis . Guzzle Guzzle integration. Separate package enaxon/reqtide-guzzle . See Guzzle requests with Reqtide . Lower-level extension points are not listed here yet: the state backends in State , the runtime adapters in Runtime , the clocks and schedulers in Time , and the identifier value types. They exist in src/ for hand-built environments, such as the one in Testing with the virtual clock . The shortest path through the surface PipelineBuilder::profile() , then partition() , attemptTimeout() and any policy methods, then compileInMemory($classifier) . $pipeline->newContext(Duration $timeout, ...) for each operation. $pipeline->execute($callable, $context) for a value or an exception, or executeDetailed() for an ExecutionCompletion . Start with Getting started if you have not run that sequence yet. Build a pipeline Everything you need at start-up. PipelineBuilder assembles and validates the configuration, compileInMemory() wires process-local state, and the result is a CompiledPipeline you reuse. Every number is yours: the builder has no defaults. PipelineBuilder Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Pipeline Fluent, immutable builder: every call returns a new builder. profile() , partition() and attemptTimeout() are required; a policy exists only if you call its method. A bad value raises InvalidConfigurationException naming the option. profile() Names and versions the configuration. Change the version when settings change; shared state is keyed by it. partition() Whose state this is: application, environment, subject and an optional tenant. One pipeline serves exactly one partition. attemptTimeout() The longest one attempt may take. Use Cooperative with the synchronous runtime. retry() Up to maxAttempts attempts, the first included. More than one needs retryBudget() and a replay-safety assertion on every call. retryBudget() A token pool that pays for retries so a failing dependency is not hammered. rateLimit() Charges cost units per dispatch, retries included. Takes the result of tokenBucket() , fixedWindow() or slidingWindow() . circuitBreaker() Judges a window of completed calls, opens at a failure share, probes after the open duration. bulkhead() At most capacity operations in flight; lease bounds a slot whose owner vanished. cooldown() A shared pause after a server-stated wait. Needs a classifier that reports waits. fallbackScope() Whether a fallback may also replace admission refusals. tokenBucket() Rate algorithm: capacity, refill tokens per interval. fixedWindow() Rate algorithm: capacity per window, aligned to the epoch or to the first hit. slidingWindow() Rate algorithm: an approximate sliding counter. lastCalls() Circuit window over the last N completed calls. timeWindow() Circuit window over a duration, kept in buckets. definition() Validates the composition and returns the immutable PipelineDefinition . compile() Compiles against a hand-built RuntimeEnvironment , for example with test helpers. compileInMemory() Compiles with the real clock, sleeping delays and process-local memory backends. There is no default classifier. CompiledPipeline Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Pipeline The reusable pipeline. It holds the state that makes limits work, so build it once and share it. It has no per-operation state. newContext() One context per operation: the total deadline, the replay-safety statement, an optional fallback, cancellation and an operation id. execute() Runs a callable through the pipeline. Returns your value, throws your own exception unchanged, or throws a Reqtide refusal. executeDetailed() Same run, but returns an ExecutionCompletion instead of throwing for business or policy outcomes. executeOperation() Like execute() for an AttemptOperation object instead of a callable. executeOperationDetailed() Like executeDetailed() for an AttemptOperation . explain() Describes the configured policies and the runtime capabilities. No I/O and no state change. InMemoryEnvironment Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Pipeline Wires the runtime for one process: system clock, a scheduler that really sleeps, secure randomness and a memory backend for each configured policy. This is what compileInMemory() uses. PipelineExplanation Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Pipeline What explain() returns: profile, enabled policies and capability notes such as physical_stop=not_guaranteed . Duration Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Value A non-negative integer count of nanoseconds. Arithmetic is checked: overflow throws and never wraps. Ratio Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Value A reduced fraction in (0, 1], compared with integers. Used for the circuit failure threshold. Run an operation Per-operation objects. A context carries the total deadline and your statement about repeating the call; your callable receives an AttemptContext . ExecutionContext Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Execution Immutable facts of one logical operation. Build it with CompiledPipeline::newContext() . Do not share one between operations. AttemptContext Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Execution Passed to your callable on every attempt: operation id, attempt id, the attempt number, the deadline, the cancellation and the attempt timeout. OperationSafety Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Execution Your statement about repeating a call. unsafe() is the default and forbids retries; the other two allow them. unsafe() The default. A pipeline that allows more than one attempt refuses to run. replayableRead() You assert that running the callable again is safe. providerIdempotentMutation() You assert that the provider deduplicates repeated mutations. ReplaySafetyContract Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Execution The written assertion behind OperationSafety . Reqtide never verifies it. forReadOnlyCallable() Builds the contract for a repeatable read. Your call is the assertion; it is not checked. OperationSafetyKind Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Execution The kind of safety an operation carries. FallbackOperation Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Fallback A fallback you attach to one operation through newContext() . It receives the original outcome. FallbackScope Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Fallback What a fallback may replace. The default replaces business outcomes only. Decide what an outcome means Policies act on the classification of each attempt, and you decide what a failure is. ExceptionClassifier is the ready-made classifier; implement OutcomeClassifier for anything it cannot express. ExceptionClassifier Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome Classifier driven by lists of exception classes. Conservative on purpose: an exception nobody listed is not retried and not replaced by a fallback, but it counts as a circuit failure. Matching is instanceof . Every method returns a new instance. create() An empty classifier. retryOn() Exception classes that may be retried. countAsCircuitFailure() Narrows circuit failures to these classes (and retryable ones). Without it every exception that is not ignored counts. ignoreForCircuit() Exception classes that say nothing about the dependency, such as validation errors. Ignore wins over a failure list. fallbackOn() Exception classes a fallback may replace. retryAfterFrom() Extracts a server-stated wait (a Duration or null ) from an exception. unacceptableValuesWhen() Marks a returned value as a failure: a circuit failure, never retried, never replaced. classify() The OutcomeClassifier contract. OutcomeClassifier Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome The contract a classifier implements. Write your own when results must be retried or classified in ways the exception lists cannot say. Outcome Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome What one attempt produced: a returned value or a thrown exception. OutcomeClassification Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome The classifier's verdict on one outcome: retry eligibility, circuit verdict, fallback eligibility, acceptability, a reason code and an optional wait lower bound. OutcomeKind Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome Whether an outcome is a value or an exception. RetryEligibility Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome Whether an outcome may be retried. FallbackEligibility Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome Whether a fallback may replace an outcome. CircuitVerdict Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome How an outcome counts for the circuit breaker. Neutral outcomes neither open nor heal it. Acceptability Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Outcome Whether an outcome is acceptable to the caller. Policy options Enums and option objects the builder takes or returns. You normally reach them through PipelineBuilder and rarely construct them yourself. BackoffMode Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Retry delay growth: constant or exponential. JitterMode Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Full draws each delay uniformly from zero to the computed delay. AttemptTimeoutRequirement Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Cooperative is the honest choice for the synchronous runtime: your code must respect the time. TransportEnforced needs a runtime that declares it. FixedWindowAnchor Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Where fixed windows start: on multiples of the window length, or at the first hit. TokenBucketOptions Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Token-bucket settings returned by PipelineBuilder::tokenBucket() . FixedWindowOptions Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Fixed-window settings returned by PipelineBuilder::fixedWindow() . SlidingCounterOptions Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Sliding-counter settings returned by PipelineBuilder::slidingWindow() . CountCircuitWindow Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Circuit window over the last N completed calls. TimeCircuitWindow Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Policy Circuit window over a duration, kept in buckets. Results of an operation execute() returns your value or throws. executeDetailed() returns the same facts as data in an ExecutionCompletion . ExecutionCompletion Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Completion Disposition, terminal outcome, per-attempt records, bounded diagnostics and a summary of counts. problem() The redacted Problem for this completion, or null when there is nothing for Reqtide to report. CompletionDisposition Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Completion How an operation ended. ExecutionSummary Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Completion Counts for one operation: dispatches, retries, outcomes, fallbacks, elapsed time and whether accounting is complete. AttemptRecord Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Completion One physical attempt: its outcome, classification and circuit verdict. Diagnostic Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Completion A bounded note about a stage and reason, such as retry / attempts_exhausted . Errors and reasons Your own exceptions come out unwrapped. A Reqtide decision is a typed exception that implements ProblemCarrier . Branch on reason() and problem() , never on message text. Problem Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error Immutable, redaction-safe report shaped after RFC 9457 problem details. It is built from code metadata only: no message, URL, header or credential can enter it. Member names and order are API; title and detail wording is not. ProblemCarrier Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error Implemented by every exception that describes a Reqtide decision. ReqtideException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception Marker for every exception raised by Reqtide itself. Your own exceptions are never wrapped in it. ReasonCode Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error The registry of stable reason codes. Keep a default branch when you match over it: new cases may be added. Show the 80 cases Retryability Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error What a caller should do with a decision: wait, retry, fix the setup or reconcile. Category Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error The kind of decision a code belongs to. Severity Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error Advisory severity, using PSR-3 level names as plain strings. Stage Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Error The lifecycle stage a diagnostic or problem is reported at. Append-only. Show the 15 cases PolicyTerminationException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception Base of the exceptions by which a policy ended the operation. AdmissionRejectedException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A circuit, bulkhead or rate limit refused the call. Your callable did not run. AdmissionDeferredException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A refusal that carries a positive wait, for example a cooldown or a Retry-After that cannot be honored. ImpossibleAdmissionException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The request can never be admitted, for example a rate cost above the capacity. DeadlineExceededException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The total deadline passed. A late value is replaced by this. OperationCancelledException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The operation was cancelled through its cancellation. FallbackFailedException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The fallback itself threw. getPrevious() is the fallback exception. InvalidConfigurationException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A value, option or combination is invalid. Raised before anything is dispatched. CapabilityMismatchException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A configured behavior needs a capability the selected runtime, scheduler or backend does not declare. InfrastructureException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception Base of clock and state-backend failures. They are never hidden as success. StateUnavailableException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A state backend could not be reached. The protected call does not run. StateCapacityExceededException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A memory backend reached its ceiling and rejected a new partition. CorruptStateException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A state backend found corrupt state. IndeterminateStateException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The result of a state-backend operation is indeterminate. ClockFailureException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception A clock could not be read. ClassificationFailedException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception The classifier itself threw. PreparationFailedException Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Exception Attempt preparation or replay verification failed. Nothing was dispatched. Events Core emits its own typed events and has no telemetry-library dependency. Pass an EventSink to compileInMemory() . A failing sink never replaces your result. EventSink Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry Receives every event. Implement it to log or count. Event Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry Base contract of all events: each names its operation and profile version. EventSeverity::of() gives an event a severity. RetryScheduled Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry A retry was scheduled after a delay. AdmissionRejected Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry A policy refused an attempt. policyKind and reasonCode say which. AttemptCompleted Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry An attempt settled, with its circuit verdict. OperationCompleted Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry The operation ended, with its disposition. CircuitTransitioned Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry The circuit changed state. BackendErrorObserved Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry A state backend reported an error. AccountingDegraded Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry A bookkeeping step after the call failed or was stale. reasonCode says which. NullEventSink Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry The default sink: discards every event. EventSeverity Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Telemetry Severity of an event, with PSR-3 level names. Test helpers In the Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing namespace, shipped in the same package. For tests only: never call them from production code. See Testing with the virtual clock . VirtualClock Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing A clock that moves only when you move it. Starts at a fixed date so a test gives the same answer every day. VirtualDelayScheduler Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing Advances a VirtualClock instead of sleeping, and records every requested delay. ControlledRuntimeAdapter Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing A synchronous runtime driven by scripts: return a value, throw, advance the clock or request cancellation per dispatch. DispatchScript Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing One scripted dispatch. RecordingEventSink Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing Stores every event for assertions. SeededRandomSource Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing Seeded randomness: same seed, same sequence. ScriptedRandomSource Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing Returns a queued list of integers and fails loudly when it runs out. Redis package: set up shared state These classes belong to the separate package enaxon/reqtide-redis , namespace Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis . It is not released and not on Packagist. See Shared state with Redis . The host creates the registry and the ACL users. The application only builds the environment. RedisEnvironment Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis The shared-state counterpart of InMemoryEnvironment . It builds a RuntimeEnvironment whose policies live in one Redis registry, for exactly the policies the definition configures. Creating it opens no connection. for() Returns the environment to pass to PipelineBuilder::compile() . The clock of every decision is the Redis server clock. explain() Adds the facts of the shared state to the usual explanation: state scope, I/O timeout, TLS, registry identity and ceilings. No I/O, and no host, password, prefix or secret in the output. RedisRegistry Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis The immutable identity of one registry, the isolation unit of shared state: a key prefix, an identity, two ceilings and a secret of 32 bytes or more. The object creates nothing in Redis. RedisRegistryAdmin Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis The only way to create, check or retire a registry. The host calls it with its administration connection. The application never does. initialize() Creates the registry, or confirms an identical one. A different identity or different ceilings are refused. Refused on a server older than Redis 7.0 and on a prefix that still holds leftovers of a retired registry. verify() Checks presence, identity, schema and counters. Writes nothing. retire() Deletes the registry metadata. Partition and record keys stay until purgeRetired() removes them. purgeRetired() Removes the keys a retired registry left behind. Refuses to run while the registry exists. Returns the number of keys removed. inspectServer() Reads maxmemory-policy and maxmemory . Needs CONFIG GET and INFO . Refuses a server older than Redis 7.0. RedisConnectionOptions Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Immutable options for one direct Redis connection. Both timeouts are required. There is no retry option and no way to turn off certificate or host name checks. The password never appears in var_dump or in an exception message. RedisAcl Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis The Redis ACL rules the library needs, and no more. Print them and pass them to ACL SETUSER . execution() Rules for the application user: the shipped scripts and their commands, only on keys under the registry prefix. administration() The same rules plus SCAN , UNLINK , CONFIG GET and INFO , for the host. RegistryStatus Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis What a registry administration call found or did: whether it created the registry, the authority watermark, and the counts against the ceilings. ServerPolicyReport Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis What the server does when it runs out of memory. protectsState() is true for noeviction or no memory limit, false for a policy that can delete keys, and null when the policy could not be read. Redis package: operate Run these from a worker, a scheduler or an operator command, with a connection that has the administration rules. The library never calls them for you. RedisRegistryMaintenance Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Bounded cleanup of a registry. One step looks at no more than 128 candidates and no more than 128 finished records. Nothing runs it for you. step() Runs one step and returns what it removed and whether more may be found. Call again until more is false. RedisCircuitAdmin Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Operator actions on circuit breaker partitions. A reset is never called by the library. reset() Closes the circuit, discards its history and makes every active permit stale. Returns the new generation, or null when the partition does not exist. Redis package: backends One backend for each policy. RedisEnvironment::for() builds them for you. Create one yourself only to pass it to ReqtideClient::withBackends() . The third constructor argument is a seam for tests: leave it out. Each backend sends one server-side script call for each step, and a command is never sent twice. RedisRateLimitBackend Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Shared token bucket, fixed window and sliding counter. RedisCircuitBackend Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Shared circuit breaker. Open and half-open state, active permits and count history are never reclaimed or evicted. RedisConcurrencyBackend Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Shared bulkhead with leases. Active owners are never evicted. RedisRetryBudgetBackend Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Shared retry budget. A balance is never reclaimed or evicted. RedisCooldownBackend Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Redis Shared cooldown. An extension never shortens an existing pause. Guzzle package These classes belong to the separate package enaxon/reqtide-guzzle , namespace Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle . It is not released and not on Packagist. See Guzzle requests with Reqtide . A 4xx or 5xx status is a response, never an exception. ReqtideClient Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle The entry point. It owns its cURL handlers and runs each operation, with all its attempts, through one compiled pipeline. inMemory() A client with process-local state. withBackends() A client whose state lives in the backends you pass, for example the Redis backends. Pass a backend for every policy the pipeline configures. uri() Resolves a reference against the base URI (RFC 3986). The base URI is not a security boundary: an absolute reference replaces the host. send() Runs the operation and returns the response, or throws the failure of the operation. The timeout covers every attempt and every wait. sendDetailed() Returns an ExecutionCompletion with the summary of attempts and decisions instead of throwing. sendAsync() Returns a lazy promise. The operation runs when you call wait() , one operation at a time. explain() Describes the compiled pipeline of the synchronous runtime, or of the promise runtime with true. No transfer, no state change. GuzzleConfiguration Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle The options of the client: the classifier, a short list of Guzzle client defaults and an optional base URI. Any other client default is refused by name. HttpOperation Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle One logical HTTP operation: an absolute request and your explicit statement of whether and how it may be sent again. Nothing is guessed from the method or a header. read() For GET and HEAD. You state that repeating the request has no side effect, for how long, and which outcomes may be retried. idempotentMutation() For POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE that the server deduplicates by an identity. The same body and the same identity are sent on every attempt, and the identity is checked before each one. single() Sent once, never repeated. Core refuses it in a pipeline that retries more than once. metadata() The bounded facts passed to the pipeline: the safety statement and the descriptor attributes. HttpOutcomeClassifier Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle Decides which statuses and cURL errors are retried, count against a circuit, may fall back or set a cooldown. Every method returns a new instance. starter() A documented starting set. It is not a recommendation for every API. empty() Retries and falls back on nothing and reads no Retry-After . retryableOutcomeCodes() The reason codes that can be retried. Pass them to read() and idempotentMutation() . An empty list never retries. RetryAfter Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle A strict reader of Retry-After : seconds or an HTTP date. A value that cannot be read gives no wait. read() Reads the header lines against the given wall time. The greatest valid delay wins. RetryAfterReading Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Guzzle What the Retry-After lines of one response say. A null delay means no lower bound, never zero. Previous Guzzle requests with Reqtide Next Limits and non-guarantees"},{"t":"Limits and non-guarantees","u":"guides/limits.html","d":"An honest list of what the alpha does not do, what Reqtide never promises, what it commits to, and what is only planned.","h":[["alpha-status","Alpha status"],["what-the-alpha-does-not-do","What the alpha does not do"],["what-reqtide-commits-to","What Reqtide commits to"],["separate-packages-and-plans","Separate packages and plans"],["versioning-and-compatibility","Versioning and compatibility"],["related","Related"]],"x":"Alpha status Nothing is released. There is no tagged version, and the package is not on Packagist (see Install ). Any public name or behavior may change without notice while the project is alpha. The core package enaxon/reqtide keeps its state in one process. Two separate packages exist in the development repository and are described at the end of this page, and in Shared state with Redis and Guzzle requests with Reqtide . A plan is not a promise. Tests and examples were run in Docker on the command line with PHP 8.3, 8.4 and 8.5. PHP-FPM, Apache and other web server setups were not run. A passing local run is not a support or production claim. No speed, capacity or latency claims are made. The only figures are the rough cost numbers in Guzzle requests with Reqtide , measured once on one laptop with large noise. What the alpha does not do Limit What it means for you State lives in one PHP process. Under PHP-FPM a pipeline built inside a request starts every request with a full quota and a closed circuit. Limits do not add up across requests, workers or servers. Use it where one process lives long enough to matter (a worker, a CLI job, a daemon), or use the separate Redis package, which is not released (see Shared state with Redis ). No shared quota in the core package. A rate limit in the core package protects one process. Ten workers each get the full limit. A shared quota needs the separate Redis package. A running callback is not interrupted. The total deadline is checked between steps. If your callable runs past the deadline, it runs to the end and the late result is reported as DeadlineExceeded . The synchronous runtime cannot enforce an attempt timeout, so you declare it as Cooperative and your code must stop itself. A bulkhead lease is not a physical stop. When a lease expires, the callback may still be running. The permit is free again, but the work is not stopped. Rate charges are not refunded. A call cancelled after admission and before dispatch still used its unit. Quota used is not the same as requests sent. No exactly-once execution. A call may run more than once, or once with an unknown result. Reqtide reports what it knows; it cannot make your operation idempotent. No retry of anything you did not allow. A retry needs your assertion that repeating the call is safe, a retry budget and a retryable exception class. Reqtide never infers safety, and it has no HTTP knowledge. No defaults. You choose every timeout, limit, window and delay. The numbers in the examples make behavior visible and are not recommendations. No failover guarantees. The Redis package keeps its state in Redis memory. A restart without persistence, a flush or a failover loses it. Strict failover guarantees are out of scope, and Sentinel, Cluster and Valkey are not supported. Not an HTTP client, a queue or edge protection. Reqtide wraps a callable. It does not send requests, store work for later or defend a public endpoint. Not in the first release. Hedged requests, cache revalidation, a failed-work queue and waiting queues. What Reqtide commits to These hold for the local part and are covered by its tests. One operation, one deadline. A retry whose delay cannot fit in the remaining time is not started. Your exceptions come out unwrapped. Reqtide's own refusals are typed exceptions that carry a machine-readable reason. See Error handling . Nothing is retried unless you say so. Retrying needs your safety assertion, a retry budget and a retryable exception class. A permit is released at most once, or ends by lease expiry. A circuit permit or a concurrency permit is released on success, on exception and on cancellation. If a release fails, Reqtide records the failure and the permit ends when its lease expires. Failing state is not hidden. If a state backend fails, the protected call does not run, and Reqtide does not invent fallback behavior. A failing observer or bookkeeping step after the call never replaces your result. Time is monotonic and integer-based. A clock that steps backwards never refills a quota. State is bounded. At its ceiling a memory backend frees expired records, then evicts the oldest bookkeeping that protects nothing, then rejects new partitions. It never evicts an active permit, a budget balance or an open circuit. Separate packages and plans Item State Shared state in Redis for rate limit, circuit, bulkhead, retry budget and cooldown ( enaxon/reqtide-redis ) Exists in the development repository. Guide: Shared state with Redis . Not released, not on Packagist. Run only in local Docker with PHP 8.3 to 8.5 on the command line, Redis 7.4.11 and 8.2.10. Sentinel, Cluster, Valkey and PHP-FPM are not supported. Guzzle integration ( enaxon/reqtide-guzzle ) Exists in the development repository. Guide: Guzzle requests with Reqtide . Not released, not on Packagist. Run only in local Docker with PHP 8.3 to 8.5 on the command line and Guzzle 7.15.5. PHP-FPM and PSR-18 are not supported. PSR-18, PSR-15, Symfony HTTP and framework registration Possible later Webhook delivery and verification Idea only Headless-CMS recipes Idea only Each planned item needs its own design and tests first. Watch CHANGELOG.md for what ships. Versioning and compatibility Semantic versioning. Before 1.0 a minor version can change the API. Every break is listed in CHANGELOG.md . From 1.0 only a major version breaks it. One version for the three packages. enaxon/reqtide , enaxon/reqtide-redis and enaxon/reqtide-guzzle are released together, and each adapter requires exactly the same version of the core. What the promise covers. Public classes, interfaces, enums, constants and methods that are not marked @internal ; the string values of ReasonCode and the members of the Problem JSON, including its type identifier. A new reason code is not a break, so handle unknown codes. What it does not cover. Anything marked @internal , the test helper internals, the Redis key layout and Lua scripts (the registry schema version is checked and a mismatch is refused), and the text of messages. Do not extend. Classes are final . The two abstract exception bases are for catching, not for extending. Related Choosing policies shows each protection and what it refuses. Shared state with Redis and Guzzle requests with Reqtide are the guides for the two separate packages. FAQ answers the common questions in short form. Previous API reference Next Comparison with other libraries"},{"t":"Comparison with other libraries","u":"guides/comparison.html","d":"A behavior comparison with six PHP libraries, written and run by the Reqtide author, with every caveat that applies.","h":[["what-was-compared","What was compared"],["caveats","Caveats"],["how-to-read-it","How to read it"],["reproduce","Reproduce"]],"x":"What was compared The harness lives in benchmarks/comparison/ in the repository. It has 21 scenarios written without any library's name in them, such as \"the total deadline spans retries and waits\" or \"a late release cannot free someone else's permit\". Each scenario was run against a pinned release of six other libraries through a small adapter, and against Reqtide's public API. All time is virtual, so nothing sleeps and results repeat. A scenario is graded only for libraries whose scope can express it. A rate limiter is not graded on retries. That is why most cells are \"not applicable\". Library (version tested) Scope as exercised Pass Fail N/A or not expressible Reqtide (working tree, unreleased) A pipeline of all policies, memory backends, virtual clock 18 1 0 symfony/rate-limiter 8.1.6 Rate limiting 3 1 15 symfony/http-client 8.1.8 Retry and throttling clients 2 6 11 caseyamcl/guzzle_retry_middleware 3.0.0 Retry middleware 3 3 13 spatie/guzzle-rate-limiter-middleware 2.2.0 Rate-limit middleware 1 2 16 ackintosh/ganesha 4.0.0 Circuit breaker 3 4 12 matheus85/duat 0.2.0 Callable pipeline 6 6 7 Two more scenarios are informational and not counted. Rows do not add up to the same total, and counts are not comparable across rows. A library with a narrow scope has few graded scenarios by design. Caveats The same author wrote everything. The scenarios, the adapters for the other libraries and the Reqtide adapter come from one person, and the scenarios came from the project's own gap research. Scenarios may favor what Reqtide is built to do. Some Reqtide results rest on choices the adapter made. Reqtide has no defaults, so the adapter picked its values. Six scenarios (retry budget size, POST not retried by default, abandoned probe, late release, typed rejections, clock regression) depend on such choices or on a lenient reading, and the harness flags each one. The other libraries ran on their own defaults. S15 is the one Reqtide failure, and it is by design. A request cancelled after admission and before dispatch keeps its rate unit, because Reqtide never refunds a rate charge. The scenario counts that as a failure. The other libraries could not run the same sub-case, so this row is not like for like. Two of them under-count quota when the limiter sits outside the retry layer, and are graded as composition-dependent. S12 is contestable. The scenario says peak concurrency must never exceed one. No lease-based bulkhead can satisfy that literally: once a lease expires, the first callback may still be running when the next one is admitted. The cell is graded on whether a third caller is let in while the second is running. A passing cell is narrow. It means the expected behavior was seen in that one scenario with that configuration. Only process-local memory and a virtual clock were exercised. The page says nothing about speed, shared state, real transports or production readiness. For ganesha only its APCu adapter ran, against an in-process stand-in for the APCu functions. Versions move. Other libraries ship fixes. Results describe the versions in the table on the day of the run. How to read it If you need only one protection, use the library built for it. A dedicated rate limiter or circuit breaker may fit better than a pipeline. Reqtide's pitch is the combination: one fixed order of decisions, one deadline across all of them, typed results, and tests under virtual time. If a difference in the table matters to you, run the scenario against your own configuration. Reproduce The harness needs PHP 8.4 or newer, because two of the compared libraries require it. From a clone, with Docker, install the pinned dependencies once and then run it: The harness README has the details. See also Limits . Previous Limits and non-guarantees Next FAQ"},{"t":"FAQ","u":"guides/faq.html","d":"Short answers to common questions about install, behavior, errors, testing and contributing.","h":[["install-and-status","Install and status"],["behavior","Behavior"],["errors","Errors"],["testing","Testing"],["using-these-docs","Using these docs"],["contributing","Contributing"]],"x":"Install and status Is it on Packagist? No. The package enaxon/reqtide is not published, and there is no tagged release. Install it from a clone as described in Install . Is it ready for production? No claim is made. It is alpha: names and behavior may change, and the core package keeps its state in one process. It was run on the command line in Docker, not under PHP-FPM. See Limits . Which PHP versions work? PHP 8.3 or newer on a 64-bit build. The tests and examples were run in Docker on 8.3, 8.4 and 8.5. Does it need any other packages? No. composer.json requires only php and php-64bit . The test helpers are in the same package. Does it work with Guzzle, PSR-18 or Symfony HTTP Client? A Guzzle integration, enaxon/reqtide-guzzle , exists as a separate package in the development repository. It is not released, not on Packagist, and was run only in local Docker with Guzzle 7.15.5 and PHP 8.3 to 8.5 on the command line. PSR-18 and Symfony HTTP Client have no integration: you wrap a call made with one of those clients in a pipeline yourself. See Guzzle requests with Reqtide and Limits . Is there Redis support? Not in the core package, whose state lives in the memory of one PHP process. A separate package, enaxon/reqtide-redis , shares state between processes. It exists in the development repository, is not released and not on Packagist, and was run only in local Docker with Redis 7.4.11 and 8.2.10 and PHP 8.3 to 8.5 on the command line. Sentinel, Cluster, Valkey and PHP-FPM are not supported. See Shared state with Redis and Limits . Behavior Does it retry a POST? Only if you tell it that repeating the call is safe. A retry needs three things: your replay-safety assertion for the call, a retry budget, and a retryable exception class. Reqtide never infers safety and has no HTTP knowledge. See Getting started . Are limits shared across PHP-FPM workers or servers? No. State is per process. A pipeline built inside a request starts every request with a full quota and a closed circuit. See Limits . Does it stop a callback that runs too long? No. The deadline is checked between steps. A result that arrives after the deadline is reported as DeadlineExceeded , but your code has already run to the end. Declare the attempt timeout as Cooperative and make your code stop itself. Does it guarantee exactly-once execution? No. Design the operation to be safe to repeat, or do not retry it. Why are there no default timeouts or limits? A default would be a guess about your dependency. You pick every number, and the builder tells you which required decision is missing. Why was my rate limit charged for a call that never ran? A rate charge is never refunded. If a call is cancelled after admission and before dispatch, its unit stays used. See Limits . Errors How do I tell my own exceptions from Reqtide's decisions? Your exceptions come out unwrapped. A refusal by Reqtide is a ReqtideException , and the ones that describe a policy decision also implement ProblemCarrier , with a stable ReasonCode and a redacted Problem . Branch on those, not on message text. See Error handling . What does rate.exhausted or circuit_open mean? The rate limit or the circuit breaker refused the call before your code ran. The Problem says whether to wait, retry or fix the setup. See Error handling . Testing How do I test retries without sleeping? Use the virtual clock and the scripted runtime from Enaxon\\Reqtide\\Testing . Time moves only when you move it, and each attempt returns what you script. See Testing . Can I use the test helpers in production code? They are for tests. They ship in the same package, so they are also in production installs, but production code should not call them. Using these docs Do the code examples really run? Yes. Every PHP code block and every output block comes from a script in examples/ that the project runs and compares with recorded output. A check fails when a block on a page is not an excerpt of an executed script. The check is not a support claim: it ran in Docker on the command line. Where is the full list of classes and methods? The API reference . Its signature listings are generated from the real classes in src/ and checked against them. Can I read the docs offline? Yes. The docs/ folder is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no build step and no external requests. Open docs/index.html in a browser. Contributing How do I report a bug or ask for a feature? Open an issue . Do not include secrets or production data. Report security problems privately, as described in the security policy . Previous Comparison with other libraries"}];
